{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_015TDypiaHdn3BghHMyYcM6F","result":"<file path=\"./context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"400\" end=\"738\" returned=\"339\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   400|\n   401|**Why this exists.** Some runtimes' targeted-edit operations have been observed to fail with `'ascii' codec can't encode character ...` errors when the `new_string` contains non-ASCII codepoints (accented vowels in Spanish / French / Portuguese / German, em dashes, smart quotes, etc.). The same failure mode has been observed to **destroy the target file** (the tool truncates the file before catching the encode exception, so a failed edit zeroes out the prior content). Until the runtime is fixed, targeted-edit cannot be trusted for SPEC_FILEs that may contain non-ASCII text.\n   402|\n   403|**Detection (at Phase B entry).** If ANY of the following is true, apply this fallback for **every** section write in the run:\n   404|- The detected output language (per Step 2) is not English.\n   405|- Any input source or `_extractions/*.md` file contains a character with codepoint > 127.\n   406|- Detection is unreliable / ambiguous — default to this fallback. The cost is bounded.\n   407|\n   408|**Per-section flow under fallback:**\n   409|\n   410|1. **Read** SPEC_FILE from disk — this is the current state (skeleton + sections populated so far).\n   411|2. **Generate** this section's body in working memory using the inputs from Phase B step 1.\n   412|3. **Compose** the full file payload in working memory:\n   413|   - header / metadata block (verbatim from disk)\n   414|   - all previously populated sections (verbatim from disk)\n   415|   - this section, freshly populated\n   416|   - remaining stubs (verbatim from disk)\n   417|4. **Write** the composed payload via the full-file replace operation (`Write` in Claude Code, `replace` in other harnesses). One call, full file content.\n   418|5. **Update** `_progress.json`: set `sections.{section_name}: complete`.\n   419|6. **Drop** everything from working memory before the next section. SPEC_FILE on disk is the source of truth; do not carry composed content forward.\n   420|\n   421|The per-section discipline is unchanged: one section populated per inference call, working memory bounded, prior section bodies not held in context. What changes is the **tool** (full-file replace, not targeted-edit) and the **payload size** (the whole file rather than one section's diff).\n   422|\n   423|**Tradeoffs:**\n   424|- Per-call output grows from ~2K tokens (first section) to ~30K tokens (last section). Cumulative ~5× the targeted-edit path. Still well within gateway limits — nowhere near the 1M-token failure mode Section 10 was designed to prevent.\n   425|- Full-file replace is **atomic**: a failed write leaves the prior file intact. This is *safer* than the destructive failure mode of the broken targeted-edit operation.\n   426|- No `old_string` validation. Mitigation: the agent reads SPEC_FILE immediately before composing the payload (step 1), so it rebuilds from disk truth, not stale working memory. Skipping step 1 is a violation — the read is what replaces validation.\n   427|\n   428|**Hard rules under fallback (from §10.5, still in force):** one section per inference call; never `Bash` + `sed` / `awk` / `python3 <<EOF` / `cat >` to mutate SPEC_FILE (the full-file replace operation is allowed, shell-based mutations are not); never compose multiple sections in memory before the write.\n   429|\n   430|**When the runtime issue is resolved:** delete this subsection and return to the targeted-edit path defined in 10.4 and 10.5. The runtime fix is a two-line change: (1) UTF-8 encoding in the edit tool's write path, (2) atomic-write semantics so failed edits leave the prior file intact.\n   431|\n   432|---\n   433|\n   434|#### 10.5.2 Safe-Write Protocol (mandatory defensive backup for SPEC files)\n   435|\n   436|**Why this exists.** Even with the discipline in §10.5 and the fallback in §10.5.1, a single bad targeted-edit call has been observed to truncate a SPEC file to 0 bytes mid-run (authored work wiped). The Safe-Write Protocol adds a `.bak` snapshot and a destructive-edit refusal gate so a single bad call cannot destroy authored work.\n   437|\n   438|**Applies to:** any skill writing to `RESEARCH-SPEC-*.md`, `PLAN-SPEC-*.md`, `IMPL-STATE-*.md`, `REVIEW-SPEC-*.md`, or any other SPEC-shaped artifact the skill declares.\n   439|\n   440|**Rule 1 — Defensive backup before any large edit:**\n   441|\n   442|Whenever you must edit a SPEC file larger than 20 KB AND the edit changes more than 30% of the file's lines:\n   443|\n   444|1. Compute: `lines_to_change` (new content vs current).\n   445|2. If `lines_to_change > 0.30 * total_lines AND file_size_kb > 20`:\n   446|   - `cp {target_file} {target_file}.bak.{ISO_TIMESTAMP}` via a single Bash call.\n   447|   - Proceed with the edit.\n   448|   - On success (post-edit file is non-empty AND > 50% of pre-edit size): delete the `.bak` file.\n   449|   - On failure or suspected truncation (post-edit file is < 50% of pre-edit size): restore from `.bak`, then emit a structured event:\n   450|     ```json\n   451|     {\"event\":\"safe_write_restore\",\"file\":\"<path>\",\"reason\":\"post_edit_too_small\",\"pre_size\":<N>,\"post_size\":<M>}\n   452|     ```\n   453|\n   454|**Rule 2 — Never `replace_all=true` on SPEC files.** Reinforces §10.5 \"Section population: use `Edit`.\" Use targeted, anchored Edits with non-empty `old_string`. If a full rewrite is genuinely needed, write to a new file first, then `mv` atomically.\n   455|\n   456|**Rule 3 — Refuse destructive `replace` calls (empty / too-short `old_string`):**\n   457|\n   458|Before any `replace` / `Edit` whose `old_string` is empty (`\"\"`) or shorter than 10 characters: REFUSE the operation. Emit:\n   459|\n   460|```json\n   461|{\"event\":\"safe_write_refuse\",\"reason\":\"old_string_too_short_or_empty\",\"file\":\"<path>\",\"old_string_length\":<N>}\n   462|```\n   463|\n   464|This is the direct guard for whole-file overwrites caused by under-specified anchors.\n   465|\n   466|**Rule 4 — Stray `.bak` cleanup at FIRST ACTION:**\n   467|\n   468|In each section-shape skill's FIRST ACTION step (the `_progress.json` write described in §2), also glob `{output_path}/*.bak.*` and delete any files older than 1 hour. This bounds disk overhead from crashes that leave a `.bak` on disk.\n   469|\n   470|**Relationship to §10.5 and §10.5.1.**\n   471|- §10.5 prevents bulk-rewrite cascades from `Bash` / `sed` / `python3`.\n   472|- §10.5.1 covers the non-ASCII-truncation failure mode (full-file replace as a workaround).\n   473|- §10.5.2 (this section) is the last line of defense — even if the agent uses the correct tool with the correct intent, the `.bak` snapshot survives a runtime malfunction.\n   474|\n   475|---\n   476|\n   477|#### 10.5.3 Tool-Output Retention (context-budget hard rule)\n   478|\n   479|**Why this exists.** Running a command is a one-time cost; the expense is that its raw output then sits in the conversation and is **re-sent on every subsequent model round-trip** in the turn (and across turns until compaction). In an observed code excursion, a single tool-heavy turn (build + `npm test` + 14 edits) reached **6.9M input tokens** — not from re-reading across turns, but because verbose build/test logs and re-read source files rode along on every one of ~70 round-trips inside that one turn. The fix is not to run or log less. It is to **always execute fully, then retain only what the next round-trip needs.**\n   480|\n   481|**The rule is about retention, never about execution. Always run the full command.** What you keep resident is decided by the output's *role*:\n   482|\n   483|| Regime | Examples | Keep resident | Rationale |\n   484||--------|----------|---------------|-----------|\n   485|| **Success** | build OK, tests green, lint clean, deploy healthy, migration applied | the **verdict only** — counts, exit code, the one summary line | A passing log carries no actionable detail; the body is pure noise. Full output is never needed. |\n   486|| **Failure** | failing test, compile error, probe 5xx, healthcheck down | the **failing slice** — the error, the stack, the failing names | The signal is the failure (usually <5% of the log). The passing lines before it are noise. Trim to the failure, not the whole run. |\n   487|| **Output *is* the evidence** | stack trace under root-cause, grep map being built, scanner findings, coverage report, diff under review | full fidelity **for this turn only**, then distill → drop | Sampling here can lose correctness, so capture full once, write the conclusion to the note/artifact, then drop the raw bytes — do not let them ride into the next round-trip. |\n   488|\n   489|**The mechanism that satisfies \"I sometimes need the full log\" without paying to re-send it — redirect to a file, summarize into context, read on demand:**\n   490|\n   491|```\n   492|npm test > .runlogs/test.txt 2>&1; tail -40 .runlogs/test.txt      # context gets the tail + counts\n   493|grep -A30 \"FAIL src/work-orders\" .runlogs/test.txt                  # full detail ONLY when a failure needs it\n   494|```\n   495|\n   496|You never lose the full log — you stop re-billing it. Prefer the tool's own quiet/summary mode at the source where one exists (`jest` summary reporter, `--silent`, `pytest -q`, `gradle --quiet`, `npm --silent`), and on a run you expect to pass do **not** enable the runner's verbose / per-case-expanding mode (`--verbose`, `-vv`, `--reporter=spec`, etc.) — that prints a line per assertion and is the exact opposite of retention. Reach for verbose only after a failure, scoped to the failing target.\n   497|\n   498|**Hard rules:**\n   499|\n   500|- Never paste a large raw log (build/test/scan/probe) into the transcript when its outcome is success — emit the verdict line instead.\n   501|- On failure, emit only the failing portion; redirect the full log to a file and `grep`/`tail` it on demand.\n   502|- After any verification run, **distill the result (pass/fail counts, the exit verdict) into the working-set note or the artifact, then drop the raw output from working memory** — the same discipline §10.4.1 / §10.6 apply to re-read source.\n   503|- Do not re-read a file you just edited in the same turn to \"re-orient\" — reason from the edit result. (Observed: one service file read 4× in a single turn.)\n   504|\n   505|**Enumeration guardrail (do NOT trim these away).** When the output is an enumeration-complete deliverable — a security scan's findings, a coverage-gap list, a toolchain inventory — every item matters and must not be sampled. The rule still applies, but \"full fidelity\" lands in the **artifact/output file**, not the chat transcript: write all findings to the file, keep only the count + the file path resident. Full is retained where it is the deliverable; it is simply not re-sent on every round-trip.\n   506|\n   507|**Applies to:** any skill that runs build/test/lint/deploy/probe/scan commands inside a multi-round-trip turn. Cited by `conducting-excursion`, `implementing-code`, `fixing-bugs`, and the quick-lane `implementing-quick-change` (the quick-fix playlist runs `fixing-bugs`, so both quick lanes are covered); other tool-running skills (`bootstrapping-runtime-environment`, `launching-app`, `launching-subscription`, `tearing-down-services`, `managing-releases`, `researching-bug-fixing`, `reviewing-code`, `api-smoke-probe`, `browser-smoke-probe`, and the security/enumeration skills with the guardrail above) adopt the §10.5.3 citation as they are next touched.\n   508|\n   509|---\n   510|\n   511|\n   512|---\n   513|\n   514|## 11. Harness Output Sidecar\n   515|\n   516|**Purpose.** The Stepwise harness reads each step's declared outputs from a sidecar JSON file written at a path the harness controls. If this file is missing, the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` in `state.json`, the UI's artifact panel renders empty, and downstream steps cannot pick up output values via propagation. **Artifacts on disk are not enough** — the sidecar is the framework contract between the skill and the orchestrator.\n   517|\n   518|**Applicability.** Any skill that may be invoked by Stepwise. Standalone invocations (no orchestrator) do not need the sidecar — see the trigger gate below.\n   519|\n   520|**When this section applies.** The input prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block with an `output_file = '<absolute path>'` line. If that block is absent (e.g. standalone invocation outside Stepwise), skip the sidecar write entirely.\n   521|\n   522|---\n   523|\n   524|### 11.1 Mechanism\n   525|\n   526|```\n   527|1. Re-read the prompt's `## Run metadata` block. Copy the path on the\n   528|   `output_file = '...'` line VERBATIM. Do NOT reconstruct it from runId,\n   529|   session name, project name, or any other source — the path the harness\n   530|   wrote there is the path the harness expects to read from.\n   531|\n   532|2. Re-read the prompt's `## Output parameters` table. For EVERY row in that\n   533|   table, include a key in the JSON object:\n   534|     - key   = the parameter name from the table\n   535|     - value = the resolved value the skill produced for that output\n   536|               (typically the path to the artifact folder or file)\n   537|\n   538|3. Write that JSON object to the `output_file` path. This is the FINAL file\n   539|   write of the run — strict ordering (see Section 11.4):\n   540|     - AFTER any skill-defined output verification (e.g. file-existence checks).\n   541|     - AFTER Memory Bank session-end writes (Section 4).\n   542|     - AFTER `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   543|\n   544|4. Do NOT emit any tool call after the sidecar write. The next event in the\n   545|   transcript should be the `final_response` summary.\n   546|```\n   547|\n   548|---\n   549|\n   550|### 11.1.1 Path Correctness Rule (mandatory)\n   551|\n   552|The value reported for any output **path** parameter MUST be the **actual on-disk path the skill wrote\n   553|to** — never a re-derived input parameter, never a fresh `value_template` substitution, and never a\n   554|scope-/sibling-adjusted path that was NOT the one actually used for the write.\n   555|\n   556|```\n   557|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every path-typed output parameter:\n   558|  1. Confirm the artifact exists at the path you are about to report\n   559|     (e.g. `ls <PATH>/<EXPECTED-FILE>*` or a stat/existence check).\n   560|  2. If the path the skill actually wrote to differs from the input parameter\n   561|     for ANY reason — sibling-folder rule, scope adjustment, feature_id stripping,\n   562|     SESSION_ID derivation — report the ACTUAL written path, NOT the parameter.\n   563|  3. Reporting a parameter you did not write to is the documented cause of the\n   564|     duplicate-artifact-on-REPAIR defect: a later REPAIR run is handed the wrong\n   565|     folder, finds no prior artifact, and silently rebuilds a duplicate. (See §7.)\n   566|```\n   567|\n   568|**Worked example (sibling rule).** A planning step receives `planning_output_path =\n   569|/base/code-development/code-task-planning` but, because `feature_id` was empty and the upstream research\n   570|was scoped, Step 1 actually wrote to `/base/code-development/TICKET-001/code-task-planning/`. The sidecar\n   571|MUST report the latter (the path that holds the files), never the flat input parameter.\n   572|\n   573|> **Note for skill authors:** \"report the parameter VERBATIM\" guidance (used by some skills to avoid\n   574|> pre-resolution double-prefixing) means *do not re-prepend project/scope segments yourself* — it does\n   575|> NOT license reporting a path you did not write to. When your own logic diverges from the parameter,\n   576|> the actual written path always wins.\n   577|\n   578|---\n   579|\n   580|### 11.1.2 Verdict Fidelity Rule (mandatory)\n   581|\n   582|§11.1.1 governs path-typed outputs. This rule governs **closed-set outputs** — any output parameter\n   583|that declares an enumerated `values:` array, which in practice means every gate / verdict-gated-transition\n   584|(VGT) verdict the orchestrator routes on.\n   585|\n   586|The value reported for such a parameter MUST be **one of the declared tokens, copied character-for-character**.\n   587|\n   588|```\n   589|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every output parameter with a declared `values:` set:\n   590|  1. Re-read the allowed tokens from the prompt's `## Output parameters` table\n   591|     (or, when the table omits them, from this skill's own verdict definition).\n   592|  2. Emit one of those tokens EXACTLY: same spelling, same case, same underscores.\n   593|     No synonym, no title-casing, no pluralisation, no added qualifier, no\n   594|     translation, no \"PASSED (with notes)\".\n   595|  3. Confirm the emitted token is a member of the declared set — a literal string\n   596|     comparison, not \"close enough\". If your computed verdict does not map onto a\n   597|     declared token, that is a bug in your mapping: pick the correct declared token,\n   598|     do not invent one.\n   599|  4. Confirm the token AGREES with the artifact you just wrote. When the report /\n   600|     spec carries the verdict in its own front-matter or summary, the two MUST be\n   601|     identical. A report that says PASSED and a sidecar that says something else is\n   602|     a self-contradiction, and the sidecar is what the orchestrator acts on.\n   603|```\n   604|\n   605|**Why this is not cosmetic.** The orchestrator matches the reported token against the step's\n   606|`gate_policy` accept/reject lists. A token in neither list is **unroutable**, so the engine\n   607|**fails closed and treats it as a reject** — the only safe default, since silently passing an\n   608|unrecognised verdict through a correctness gate would be worse. The consequence is a spurious\n   609|retry loop: the upstream producer step is re-run, every step between it and the gate is reset and\n   610|re-run with it, and the retry budget is consumed. When the budget is exhausted the run escalates\n   611|to a human gate carrying a rejection that never happened.\n   612|\n   613|**Worked example (observed).** A plan-verification gate declared\n   614|`values: [PASSED, PASSED_WITH_FINDINGS, FAILED]`. The skill wrote a report whose front-matter read\n   615|`verification_verdict: PASSED`, `weighted_total: 100`, `failed_blocker_count: 0` — a clean pass — but\n   616|emitted `\"verification_verdict\": \"APPROVED\"` in the sidecar. `APPROVED` is not in the set, so the gate\n   617|failed closed, rejected, and re-ran planning plus the two steps between. A perfect-scoring plan was\n   618|re-planned because the sidecar used a synonym.\n   619|\n   620|**Robustness note.** This write is the last action of a run, often after a long generation, and may be\n   621|performed under a degraded executor — a fallback model, a smaller tier after a quota or rate-limit\n   622|failure, or a resumed session. Those are exactly the conditions under which a token gets paraphrased.\n   623|Treat the closed-set comparison in step 3 as a real check to perform at write time, not a stylistic\n   624|preference — it is cheap, and it is the only thing standing between a correct verdict and a wasted lap.\n   625|\n   626|---\n   627|\n   628|### 11.2 Example\n   629|\n   630|For a skill that declares a single output parameter named `foo_output_path`:\n   631|\n   632|```json\n   633|{ \"foo_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/<capability>/<step>\" }\n   634|```\n   635|\n   636|For a skill with multiple output parameters, include every row from the `## Output parameters` table — one key per row. The skill-specific parameter names live in each SKILL.md (Section 11.5); this section does not enumerate them.\n   637|\n   638|---\n   639|\n   640|### 11.3 Violation\n   641|\n   642|Ending a Stepwise run with a `final_response` summary that lists the generated artifacts but no sidecar write breaks output propagation. The harness MAY have a `value_template` fallback for specific outputs, but that fallback is not guaranteed for every skill / every output. Treat the sidecar write as **non-optional** whenever the `## Run metadata` block is present in the prompt — do not rely on the fallback.\n   643|\n   644|Skills whose outputs lack a `value_template` fallback fail **silently** when the sidecar is missing: downstream steps see `outputs: {}` and propagate empty values; no error surfaces in the run. This is the failure mode this section prevents.\n   645|\n   646|---\n   647|\n   648|### 11.4 Composition with Section 2 LAST ACTION\n   649|\n   650|The sidecar write extends Section 2's LAST ACTION; it does NOT replace it. Strict ordering for any Stepwise-invoked skill:\n   651|\n   652|1. All skill-specific outputs written and verified.\n   653|2. Memory Bank writes (Section 4 — `active-context.md`, `progress.md`).\n   654|3. `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   655|4. **Sidecar write** (this section) — final file write of the run.\n   656|5. `final_response` summary — no further tool calls.\n   657|\n   658|If the skill is invoked standalone (no `## Run metadata` block), step 4 is skipped. Steps 1–3 and 5 still apply.\n   659|\n   660|---\n   661|\n   662|### 11.5 Skill-Level Enforcement\n   663|\n   664|Section 11 cannot enumerate output parameter names — those are declared per-skill in the capability YAML's `outputs` block and surface in each prompt's `## Output parameters` table. Each SKILL.md MUST include a short inline callout naming its output parameters, so the agent knows which keys to put in the sidecar JSON without parsing the YAML.\n   665|\n   666|Recommended SKILL.md callout shape:\n   667|\n   668|> **Step N: Emit harness outputs sidecar — apply execution-protocol.md Section 11.**\n   669|>\n   670|> **Output parameters this skill produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   671|>\n   672|> - `<param_name_1>`: <one-line description of the resolved value, e.g. \"the resolved output folder path written in earlier steps\">\n   673|> - `<param_name_2>`: ...\n   674|>\n   675|> Example sidecar contents (illustrative):\n   676|>\n   677|> ```json\n   678|> { \"<param_name_1>\": \"/abs/path/...\", \"<param_name_2>\": \"...\" }\n   679|> ```\n   680|\n   681|The callout is intentionally short. Trigger gate, mechanism, ordering, and violation rule all live in Section 11 — the SKILL.md only names the keys.\n   682|\n   683|---\n   684|\n   685|\n   686|### 11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (universal)\n   687|\n   688|**Trigger.** This run wrote an `## Open Questions` / `## open_questions` section (any heading level, any spelling) into an artifact, AND the prompt has a `## Run metadata` block. Otherwise skip — nothing to mirror.\n   689|\n   690|**Rule.** Mirror that section into a reserved `_open_questions` key in the same sidecar JSON. This is not extra analysis: you already decided what the open questions are and wrote them down. This emits the same list in a form the orchestrator can act on.\n   691|\n   692|`_open_questions` is a RESERVED key. It is never a declared output parameter, never appears in the `## Output parameters` table, and is stripped by the harness before output resolution. Adding it never breaks propagation.\n   693|\n   694|```json\n   695|{\n   696|  \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\",\n   697|  \"_open_questions\": [\n   698|    {\n   699|      \"id\": \"PI-10\",\n   700|      \"question\": \"Which inventory slice does the pilot run on?\",\n   701|      \"subject\": \"scope\",\n   702|      \"why_it_matters\": \"Every downstream sizing estimate assumes one slice\",\n   703|      \"blocking\": true,\n   704|      \"assumption_if_unanswered\": \"one DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\",\n   705|      \"kind\": \"question\",\n   706|      \"audience\": \"client\",\n   707|      \"artifact_ref\": \"/abs/path/to/PRD-SPEC-....md#open-questions\",\n   708|      \"traces_to\": [\"EPIC-02\", \"FR-14\"]\n   709|    }\n   710|  ]\n   711|}\n   712|```\n   713|\n   714|**Fields.** `question` is the only required one. Emit every other field you actually have; omit what you do not. Never invent one — Zero-Invention applies here exactly as it does to the artifact.\n   715|\n   716|- `id` — your own local id (`PI-10`, `OQ-03`) if the section has one.\n   717|- `kind` — `question` (default) or `assumption`. See the note below; getting this right decides which verbs the operator is offered.\n   718|- `subject` — what the question is ABOUT (`scope`, `compliance`, `personas`). This is what turns a 60-row inbox into a dozen themes.\n   719|- `why_it_matters` — the consequence of leaving it open.\n   720|- `blocking` — `true` ONLY if downstream work genuinely cannot proceed. See the warning below.\n   721|- `assumption_if_unanswered` — what you actually proceeded on. **Emit this whenever you proceeded at all.** Without it, any later answer must be treated as invalidating every artifact built on the gap, because there is no basis to compare against — so a stated assumption is what keeps a confirming answer from triggering pointless rework.\n   722|- `audience` — who can answer: `client`, `architect`, `product_owner`, `internal`. `client` is the only value that can send the question out of the building.\n   723|- `artifact_ref` — absolute path (optionally `#anchor`) of the artifact that raised it.\n   724|- `traces_to` — ids the question affects (`EPIC-02`, `FR-14`, `BC-09`).\n   725|\n   726|**`kind` separates a gap from a claim.** A `question` names something UNKNOWN — the operator resolves it by supplying the value, or by accepting the assumption you proceeded on. An `assumption` names something you ASSERTED and could not verify (\"Theobald is compatible with the SAP S/4HANA landscape\", \"Fabric is licensable in Germany West Central\") — the operator resolves it by confirming it, which changes nothing because the artifact already says it, or by correcting it, which invalidates everything built on it. Emit `assumption` for anything you would have written into an `assumptions_to_validate` table. A question phrased as a statement of absence (\"No client-approved RPO exists\") is still a `question`: it needs a value supplied, not a claim confirmed. For an `assumption` you may omit `assumption_if_unanswered` — the claim IS what the work proceeded on.\n   727|\n   728|**`blocking` means the SCHEDULE, not the document.** Registry tables use `blocking: yes` to mean \"this document cannot be considered complete.\" Here it means \"downstream steps must not run until this is answered,\" and an operator has to confirm before it stops anything. If work can proceed on a stated assumption, it is `false` with an `assumption_if_unanswered` — that is the normal case.\n   729|\n   730|**Completeness.** The sidecar is complete by contract: list EVERY open question this run is raising, or omit `_open_questions` entirely. Do not emit a partial list — the orchestrator reads an unlisted question as one you resolved and stops tracking it.\n   731|\n   732|**Wording.** Copy `question` verbatim from the section you wrote. Identity is derived from the question text, so a reworded sidecar entry becomes a SECOND record instead of enriching the one already tracked.\n   733|\n   734|**Ordering.** No change to §11.4 — `_open_questions` is one more key in the same JSON, written at the same point.\n   735|\n   736|**Standalone runs.** No `## Run metadata` block means no sidecar at all (§11 trigger gate). The artifact still carries the section; nothing is lost.\n   737|\n   738|---\n</file>","isError":false}}
