{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01RJ96k1ciXtnjfKkoqN342E","result":"---\nname: analyze-context-pack-inputs\ndescription: >\n  Analyzes uploaded source code repositories, PDF documents, transcripts, and\n  markdown files to extract structured knowledge for context-pack generation.\n  Selects the applicable technology-stack blueprints first (delegating to\n  `tech-blueprints`), then produces a single multi-section analysis document\n  covering tech stack, domain terms, standards, policies, and constraints found\n  in the inputs — each section labeled with the context-pack file it feeds.\n  Activate when the task mentions \"analyze inputs\", \"context pack analysis\",\n  \"tech stack selection\", or references a project root for context-pack generation.\nmetadata:\n  author: aipods-team\n  version: 2.3.0\n  category: context-pack\n  tags: context-pack, analysis, onboarding, agent-native, tech-blueprints, parallel-fanout\n---\n\n# Analyze Context Pack Inputs\n\nReads everything under `artifacts/inputs/` (source code, PDFs, markdown, transcripts)\nand produces one structured analysis spec at\n`artifacts/outputs/context-pack-analysis/analysis.md`. That spec is the sole input to\n`generate-context-pack`, which turns each `## For <file>` section into a normative\ncontext-pack file. **This skill only extracts and structures what the inputs actually\ncontain — it never invents standards, constraints, or domain facts.**\n\n## Generation shape & protocol route\n\n**Shape: section-shape** — one multi-section spec (`analysis.md`) synthesized from large,\nunstructured inputs. Apply `execution-protocol.md` in this order, each section lazily as it\nbecomes relevant:\n\n`§2` (FIRST/LAST `_progress.json`, section-shape variant §10.1) → `§10.2` (source extraction pass) → `§10` (skeleton-first §10.3, one-section-per-`Edit` §10.4) → `§10.5` (tool discipline incl. §10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback) → `§11` (harness output sidecar). Also: `§5` (FIC monitoring), `§7` (REPAIR when `failure_feedback` present), `§9` (non-interactive), `§4` (Memory Bank — this skill opts in).\n\n`execution-protocol.md` owns all of these mechanics. This SKILL.md supplies only the\nskill-specific contract bits below (section list, cross-section dependencies, extraction\nschema). Do not inline the protocol machinery.\n\n**Where the protocol is — do not search for it.** Stepwise stages it at\n`{base_path}/context-pack/system/`:\n\n```\n{base_path}/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md              # routing rule + §2, §10.5, §11 (universal, inline)\n{base_path}/context-pack/system/execution-protocol/<NN>-<name>.md  # every other section, one file each\n```\n\nResolve each `§N` in the route above to its file via that directory listing, and read **only**\nthe sections your route selects. Reading the root file end-to-end to locate a section that\nlives in its own file is a §10.4.1 budget violation in spirit: it is ~44 KB, and the routing\ntable it exists to serve is in its first 60 lines. If the directory is genuinely absent, say so\nin `## Open Questions` and proceed — do not spend tool calls sweeping the filesystem for it.\n\n### §14 parallel fan-out — DISPATCH ALL WORKERS IN ONE TURN\n\nThis skill declares **one** §14 unit set: **Step 1.5 — one unit per project input file**,\nread-only extraction, coordinator synthesizes. Each unit is a whole file to read and distill,\nwhich is expensive enough to be worth a worker.\n\n**Step 2 Phase B is NOT fan-out eligible — this is a measured decision, not an oversight.**\nThe sixteen `for-*` sections *are* independent (each reads only its own extraction fields), so\n§10.9 would permit an `independent_sections` whitelist. It was tried and it made the run\n**slower**: serial Phase B ran at ~11s/section, and the same work fanned out ran at ~18s/section\nbecause a worker spawn costs ~30–40s of context load while composing one section is ~10s of\nactual work. Fan-out collapses *sum-of-units* toward *slowest-unit* only when the unit is large\nrelative to spawn overhead. A section body is not. Populate sections with the serial\none-section-per-`Edit` loop of §10.4.\n\nDo not re-derive this from \"the sections are independent, therefore parallel\" — independence is\nnecessary for fan-out, not sufficient. The unit has to be expensive too.\n\nFan out the Step 1.5 extraction pass whenever the harness can dispatch multiple workers in a\nsingle turn — one worker per unit. Concurrent foreground workers alone qualify;\nbackground/detached tasks are NOT required, and an absent `Parallel delegation:` advertisement\ndoes NOT force inline (see `execution-protocol.md §14 Applicability`). Determine the capability\ndirectly rather than treating a missing advertisement as unavailable.\n\n- **Dispatch all workers for the shard in ONE turn** (§14.3 step 2) — emitting one worker per\n  turn serializes them and forfeits the benefit. Workers write only their own\n  `EXTRACTIONS/{basename}.md`; the coordinator alone writes `analysis.md` and `_progress.json`.\n- Fall back to the inline extraction pass in enumeration order ONLY when the harness is\n  genuinely single-dispatch. This is a normal path, not a failure, and the output is identical.\n\nPer §12.4, worker output is input, not ground truth: the coordinator verifies each extraction\nexists and is non-empty before Phase B reads it.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Type | Required | Default | Description |\n|-----------|------|----------|---------|-------------|\n| `base_path` | string | No | `./` | Project root. All input/output paths derive from it. |\n| `selected_inputs` | string | No | — | Newline- or comma-separated list of paths under `INPUT_ROOT` that this run may read. When present it **replaces** the recursive enumeration in Step 1 (see below). When absent, all of `INPUT_ROOT` is in scope. |\n| `stack` | string | No | — | Comma-separated `blueprint_id` list, passed through to `tech-blueprints` in Step 0 (e.g. `aws,mobile-native,langgraph`). Ignored when a selection already exists. |\n| `failure_feedback` | string | No | — | REPAIR-mode directives (triggers §7). |\n\n**`selected_inputs` is a hard scope, not a hint.** The caller (Stepwise, or a capability step)\nrenders it as a `Selected inputs — analyze ONLY these paths` block in the prompt. Files under\n`INPUT_ROOT` that are not in the list are out of scope: do not read them, do not enumerate\nthem, do not cite them as a source. Their absence is not a gap to report — the human chose it.\n\nThis exists because the caller often *cannot* prune the unselected files off disk: when any\nselected input is a referenced source folder rather than an upload, the staging layer scans in\nplace and leaves the whole input tree present. The prompt is then the only carrier of the\nscope, and a run that ignores it analyzes — and bills for — material the human deselected.\n\n**STOP-GATE — abort before any generation if, and only if:**\n- No files exist under `{base_path}/artifacts/inputs/` (nothing to analyze), or\n  `selected_inputs` resolves to an empty set — write a Gap Report to the output folder and EXIT\n  per §9. Do NOT produce an empty analysis.\n\n**This is the only STOP-GATE. No blueprint outcome is a blocker.** Blueprints are\n*complementary*: they supply defaults where the project material is silent. Whatever Step 0\nreturns — a full selection, a partial one, no L1 platform, several L1 candidates, or a failed\ninvocation — you **continue** and produce `analysis.md` from the inputs you have. Sections that\nneeded a platform answer resolve to `status: pending - no evidence in inputs` and are mirrored\ninto `## Open Questions`; that is the mechanism for \"unclear in the inputs and not covered by a\nblueprint\", and it is already sufficient. Do not invent a new severity, and do not withhold the\nwhole deliverable over one unresolved dimension.\n\nThe failure this replaces: a standalone TypeScript CLI has no cloud platform, so blueprint\nselection legitimately produced no L1 — and the run refused to emit any analysis at all. An\nabsent answer to a question the project never asked is not a gap. A context pack with a marked\nhole is useful; no context pack is not.\n\n## Path resolution\n\n```\nINPUT_ROOT      = {base_path}/artifacts/inputs/\nOUTPUT_ANALYSIS = {base_path}/artifacts/outputs/context-pack-analysis/\nSPEC_FILE       = {OUTPUT_ANALYSIS}/analysis.md\nEXTRACTIONS     = {OUTPUT_ANALYSIS}/_extractions/\n```\n\nCreate `OUTPUT_ANALYSIS` if it does not exist.\n\n## Session identity\n\nDerive `SESSION_ID` per `execution-protocol.md §1` with TYPE prefix `ANALYZE`.\nREPAIR reuses the prior `SESSION_ID` parsed from the existing `ENGINEERING-AUDIT-*` /\n`analysis.md` header — never mint a new one. Do NOT construct `SESSION_ID` from a literal\ndate/UUID formula here; §1 owns the format.\n\n## Zero Invention Policy\n\nEvery line in `analysis.md` MUST trace to a specific input file (and, where the format\nallows, a line number). This is the core anti-hallucination rule for this skill:\n\n- Facts not present in the inputs do NOT appear in the analysis.\n- Industry-standard hints you infer are allowed ONLY when tagged `[INFERRED]` inline, and\n  they must also be logged in `## Open Questions` for human confirmation.\n- Never upgrade an `[INFERRED]` hint into a stated fact.\n- If a target section has no supporting evidence, emit the section with\n  `status: pending — no evidence in inputs` rather than fabricating content.\n\n## Source Precedence — project inputs outrank blueprints\n\nTwo kinds of material feed this analysis, and they are **not** equal authority:\n\n| Rank | Source | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Project inputs** — the client's brief, requirements, transcripts, and their actual repository (manifests, lockfiles, config, CI) | What this project **is**. Authoritative. |\n| 2 | **Staged blueprints** — `artifacts/inputs/tech-blueprints/` + `tech-stack-selection.md` | What a project of this shape **should** default to. Fills gaps only. |\n\n**The rule: where the two disagree on the same fact, the project input wins — always.** A\nblueprint mandating Node 22 does not overrule a client who wrote \"Node 18\". A blueprint's\nTerraform mandate does not overrule a repo with `main.bicep` in it. The blueprint describes a\nreference stack; the input describes reality, and a context pack that contradicts reality sends\nevery downstream coding agent to the wrong runtime.\n\n**Blueprints are load-bearing exactly where the inputs are silent.** That is most of a typical\nrun — the client says \"TypeScript CLI\" and says nothing about linting, test layout, secret\nhandling, or CI ordering. Fill those from the blueprint and mark them normally. Never withhold\na blueprint rule because the inputs merely failed to mention the topic; silence is not\ndisagreement.\n\n**When you supersede a blueprint rule, say so in the same bullet.** One clause, so the decision\nis auditable and nobody re-litigates it later:\n\n```\n- complete: Node 18 LTS is the target runtime. (source: `artifacts/inputs/brief.md`, line 22;\n  supersedes `node-typescript` blueprint Normative Profile `runtime: node>=22`)\n```\n\nEmit **only** the input-derived rule. Never emit both sides and never emit the blueprint value\nwith a \"but the client said…\" caveat — a context pack with two contradictory mandates is worse\nthan one with a stale rule, because the agent reading it has to guess.\n\n**Escalate a conflict to `## Open Questions` only when the input choice breaks something the\nblueprint treats as a hard floor** — an EOL or unsupported runtime, a security-baseline control,\na version below a stated minimum for a required feature. That is worth a human's attention.\nA plain version or tool difference is not: it is the client's call, already made, and logging\nit as a question buries the real questions.\n\n**Precedence within rank 1.** When two project inputs disagree, the repository beats the prose:\na lockfile or `tsconfig.json` is what actually builds, a brief is what someone intended. Note\nthe discrepancy in `## Open Questions` — that one *is* worth confirming.\n\n**Where the selection file already recorded the conflict**, prefer it: entries under\n`## Deviations from blueprint mandates` and `## Project-specific resolutions` in\n`tech-stack-selection.md` are this same rule applied earlier, with justification attached. Use\nthat justification rather than re-deriving one. See `references/input-signal-catalog.md`\n§ Tech-Blueprint Analysis for the extraction mechanics.\n\n## Status Protocol\n\nEvery extracted item carries one status: `complete` (evidence found and captured),\n`pending` (no evidence — needs a source), or `assumption` (`[INFERRED]`, needs human\nconfirmation). Assumptions and pendings are mirrored into `## Open Questions`.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2)\n\nBefore any other file write, write `_progress.json` (section-shape variant, §10.1) to\n`OUTPUT_ANALYSIS`, using `\"session_id\": \"initializing\"` and all `sections` set to\n`pending`, `skeleton_written: false`. This prevents orchestrator SIGINT.\n\nAlso load the Memory Bank per §4 Session Start (read `./context-pack/active-context.md`\nand `progress.md` if present; write `active-context.md` with status `STARTING`).\n\n### Step 0 — Ensure blueprint selection (delegates to `tech-blueprints`)\n\nRuns after the FIRST ACTION and before Step 1, so that input discovery sees a correctly staged\nfolder. **Idempotent — never re-selects when a selection already exists.**\n\n```\nSELECTION = {INPUT_ROOT}/tech-stack-selection.md\nSTAGED    = {INPUT_ROOT}/tech-blueprints/\n```\n\n1. **Selection already present** (`SELECTION` exists) → do nothing. Proceed to Step 1. This is\n   the REPAIR path and the re-run path; re-selecting would discard recorded deviations and\n   accepted trade-offs.\n2. **No selection** → **invoke `tech-blueprints`** with `base_path`, the pass-through `stack`\n   parameter, and the same `selected_inputs` scope. It writes `SELECTION` and stages the\n   selected blueprints into `STAGED`. This is the default branch and it is **not optional** —\n   see the availability rule below.\n3. **Whatever Step 0 returns, proceed to Step 1.** Record the outcome in `## Open Questions`\n   and keep going:\n   - Full selection → nothing to record; use it.\n   - **No L1 platform selected** → normal for a CLI, library, or local tool. Use the\n     L2/L3/L4 blueprints as usual. Record nothing unless the project clearly *does* deploy\n     somewhere and the platform is simply unstated — then that is a real open question.\n   - **Several L1 candidates, none chosen** → record the candidates and what would decide\n     between them; leave platform-dependent sections `pending`. Never pick one yourself.\n   - **`SELECTION` missing but `STAGED` populated** → use the staged blueprints and record the\n     missing selection file (deviations and accepted trade-offs will be absent).\n   - **The invocation failed** → record the failure and its reason; platform-dependent\n     sections resolve as `pending`.\n\n   None of these abort the run. See the STOP-GATE note in Parameters: the only blocker is an\n   empty input set.\n\n**Availability rule — branch 3 requires a failed attempt, not an assumption.** Treat\n`tech-blueprints` as AVAILABLE unless invoking it fails. \"The prompt listed only this skill\",\n\"no blueprints are staged\", and \"the inputs look simple\" are **not** unavailability: the first\nis a prompt-rendering artifact, and the other two are the exact conditions branch 2 exists to\nresolve. A run that skips Step 0 and reports `no blueprint selection` in Open Questions has not\nobserved a gap — it has created one, and every platform-dependent section (`for-tech-policy`,\n`for-coding-standards`, `for-test-standards`, `for-validation-tools`, `for-security`,\n`for-infrastructure`) resolves to `pending` as a direct result. That is the single largest\nquality loss this skill can inflict, and it is silent.\n\nDo not re-implement selection logic here. Choosing a platform requires the decision matrix and\ndisqualifiers that `tech-blueprints` owns; duplicating that judgment in this skill produces two\nsources of truth that will drift.\n\n### Step 1 — Input discovery\n\nRead `references/phase-a-input-discovery-extraction.md`. Categorize inputs by type (source-code\narchives/dirs, documentation PDFs/MD, meetings — skip MP4s, handled by `transcribe-meetings`),\nand prepare source files: extract ZIPs and PDF text using the bundled scripts in `scripts/`\n(`unzip_safe.py`, `read_pdf.py` — both stdlib-only with zip-slip protection and graceful\nfallbacks).\n\n**Determining the input set:**\n- `selected_inputs` **present** → the input set is exactly those paths, plus a recursive walk of\n  any that are directories. Do not enumerate `INPUT_ROOT`. Record the scope verbatim in\n  `## Source Summary` so a reader can tell a deselected file from a missing one.\n- `selected_inputs` **absent** → enumerate `INPUT_ROOT` recursively (the historical behavior).\n\n**Symlinks: resolve, then bound.** A staged input may be a symlink to a folder elsewhere on the\nbox. Resolve it, but never follow one whose target contains `INPUT_ROOT` — that is a cycle\n(`artifacts/inputs/source-code/inputs -> artifacts/inputs` has been observed in the wild) and\nwalking it does not terminate. Skip the cyclic entry, note it in `## Source Summary`, and carry\non with the rest; do not treat it as a fatal input error.\n\n### Step 1.5 — Source Extraction Pass (§10.2)\n\n**Staged blueprints are NOT extraction units — never distill them.** Every blueprint in\n`{INPUT_ROOT}/tech-blueprints/` already ends with a `## 14. Normative Profile` fenced YAML\nblock written in *this skill's own extraction schema*. The extraction already exists; lift it\nverbatim into the merged set (tagged `origin: blueprint` with `blueprint_id` /\n`blueprint_version`) and move on. Dispatching a worker to re-read 14 KB of blueprint prose in\norder to synthesize a file that is already the last section of that same blueprint is pure\nduplicated work — measured at ~4 minutes across three blueprints, for output the profile block\nhands over for free.\n\n**They are also excluded from the §10.2 file count.** Only *project* inputs count toward the\nthresholds below. Otherwise selecting three blueprints silently pushes a one-file project over\nthe \"more than 2 source files\" line and turns a pass that would have been skipped into the\nlongest phase of the run — which is exactly what happened the first time Step 0 fired.\n\nApply `execution-protocol.md §10.2` to the project inputs. Trigger thresholds (verbatim from\n§10.2): run the pass if **more than 2 source files**, OR **any single source file > 200 lines**,\nOR **cumulative source content > 500 lines**; otherwise SKIP and read sources directly in\nStep 2. Distill each project input into `EXTRACTIONS/{basename}.md` using this skill's\nextraction schema (one fielded bullet-list block, `line: {N}` provenance on every entry):\n\n```\ntech_signals:   [{ signal, evidence, line }]        # frameworks, runtimes, deps, versions\ndomain_terms:   [{ term, definition_or_usage, line }]\nstandards:      [{ area: coding|arch|test|security|automation, rule, line }]\nconstraints:    [{ constraint, source, line }]       # compliance, hosting, perf, org limits\ninfra:          [{ item, detail, line }]             # cloud, IaC, containers, networking\ncommands:       [{ purpose: build|test|lint|typecheck|security, command, line }]\ninventory:      [{ project_or_type, path, role, line }]\ndesign:         [{ artifact_type: journey|screen|component|state|brand|interaction|decision, name, detail, source, line }]  # UX/product surface only — journeys, screens, components, states, brand rules, verified design decisions; NO architecture or ADRs\nopen_questions: [{ question, why }]                  # gaps, unparseable files, ambiguities\n```\n\n**Provenance fields, on every entry:** `origin: input|blueprint` — which rank the entry came\nfrom (§ Source Precedence). Blueprint entries additionally carry `blueprint_id` and\n`blueprint_version`. Entries that won a conflict carry `supersedes: \"<blueprint_id> <field>:\n<value>\"`. Without `origin` the coordinator cannot tell rank 1 from rank 2 at merge time and\nthe precedence rule is unenforceable — a blueprint version silently outlives the client's.\n\nPhase B reads `EXTRACTIONS/*.md` only — raw sources are dropped from working memory\n(§10.2). REPAIR reuses unchanged extractions.\n\n**Conflict reconciliation happens at merge, not in a worker.** A §14 worker sees one input file\nand cannot know whether another file contradicts it, so workers extract and tag `origin`; the\n**coordinator** applies § Source Precedence across the merged set before Phase B composes\nanything. This is the same coordinator-owns-the-merge rule as §14.3 step 4.\n\n### Step 2 — Generate `analysis.md` (§10)\n\nRead `references/phase-b-analysis-section-generation.md` and\n`references/input-signal-catalog.md` (the what-to-look-for → which-context-pack-file\nmapping tables). Apply §10 Phase A then Phase B.\n\n**Phase A — skeleton-first (§10.3, within 5 tool calls):** `Write` `SPEC_FILE` with header\n+ one stub per section (`status: pending - will be generated`, ASCII hyphen only). Flip\n`_progress.json.skeleton_written: true`.\n\n**Phase B — populate each section from the extractions that feed it,** then flip\n`sections.{name}: complete`. **Section list, in template order:**\n\n```\nfor-domain → for-arch-standards → for-tech-policy → for-coding-standards →\nfor-automation-standards → for-test-standards → for-security → for-constraints →\nfor-customer-background → for-env-config-and-config-management →\nfor-best-practices → for-infrastructure → for-validation-tools →\nfor-project-inventory → for-codebase-map → for-ux-design →\nsource-summary → open-questions\n```\n\n**Phase B ordering — triage first, then two passes.** Before composing anything, classify every\n`for-*` section against the extraction fields that feed it (per\n`references/input-signal-catalog.md`):\n\n1. **Zero-evidence sections** — *every* feeding field is empty across *every* extraction. These\n   have exactly one correct body: the section heading, its subsection headings, and\n   `status: pending - no evidence in inputs` under each, plus one line in `## Open Questions`.\n   There is no judgment to exercise and nothing to compose. Write them in **one** `Edit`\n   covering all of them, and flip their `_progress.json` entries in one update.\n2. **Evidence-bearing sections** — at least one feeding field has an entry. Populate these with\n   the serial one-section-per-`Edit` loop of §10.4. Do not fan them out; see the §14 note above\n   for why that was measured slower.\n\nThen `source-summary` and `open-questions`, serially, last.\n\n**Why triage:** a section with no evidence still costs a full inference turn under the plain\n§10.4 loop — roughly 10 seconds each, for output that is a fixed string. On a thin input set\nthat is most of the section list (a measured run: 6 of 16 sections wholly zero-evidence, ~24 of\n~52 subsections). Batching them changes no output byte; it removes turns that decide nothing.\nThis is a scope narrowing of §10.4 for this skill only, permitted because these sections carry\nno content decision — do **not** batch an evidence-bearing section to save a turn.\n\n**Cross-section dependencies:** all `for-*` sections are independent (each reads only its\nown extraction fields). `source-summary` reads the SOURCE_LOG. `open-questions` is a\nconsolidator — it reads every populated section's `pending`/`assumption` items. Read\n`SPEC_FILE` for prior state ONLY for those two consolidators (§10.4).\n\n**Tool discipline (§10.5):** skeleton via `Write`, sections via `Edit`; **never** `Bash`\n+ `sed`/`awk`/`python3 <<EOF`/`cat >` to mutate `SPEC_FILE`. If inputs or extractions\ncontain any codepoint > 127 (non-English content is common here), apply the §10.5.1\nfull-file-`Write` fallback for every section in the run.\n\n### REPAIR mode (§7)\n\nIf a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block is present: load the existing `analysis.md`, parse\nfeedback into per-section directives, rewrite ONLY targeted sections, preserve the rest\nverbatim, reuse unchanged extractions, bump the spec version, and record the delta in the\naudit. Do NOT regenerate from scratch.\n\n### LAST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2, §4, §11)\n\nIn this strict order (§11.4):\n1. Verify `analysis.md` exists and every section is `complete` or explicitly `pending`.\n2. Memory Bank session-end (§4): overwrite `./context-pack/active-context.md` with final\n   state; append one `progress.md` milestone row. **Artifact type: `analysis-sections`**\n   (e.g. `17 analysis-sections`).\n3. Flip `_progress.json` to `COMPLETED`.\n4. **Harness output sidecar (§11)** — apply `execution-protocol.md §11` **only if** the\n   prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block with `output_file` and a `## Output\n   parameters` table. Write one key per declared parameter (typically the analysis output\n   path). In the standard `build-context-pack` step this skill is an intra-step producer\n   and `generate-context-pack` writes the step's sidecar — if no `## Output parameters`\n   table is present, skip the sidecar per §11's trigger gate.\n5. `final_response` — no tool calls after the sidecar write.\n\n## Error handling\n\n- `tech-blueprints` returns no L1, several L1 candidates, an unknown `blueprint_id`, or fails\n  outright: **continue**. Record the outcome in `## Open Questions`, leave platform-dependent\n  sections `pending`, and analyze everything the inputs do support. Never guess a platform —\n  a fabricated one yields a confidently wrong context pack — but never treat its absence as\n  fatal either. Those are different mistakes and only the first is worth refusing over.\n- `tech-blueprints` unavailable: follow Step 0 branch 3. No blueprint outcome is fatal.\n- ZIP/PDF extraction failures: log and continue with remaining inputs; add the failure to\n  `## Open Questions`. If `pdfminer.six` and `pdftotext` are both unavailable, note the\n  degraded raw-text extraction and tell the user to `pip install pdfminer.six`.\n- No inputs in a category: skip it and note in `## Source Summary`.\n- Empty input set overall: STOP-GATE above — Gap Report + EXIT.\n\n## Reference files\n\n- `references/phase-a-input-discovery-extraction.md` — enumeration, script usage, the §10.2 extraction-pass mechanics for this skill.\n- `references/phase-b-analysis-section-generation.md` — per-section content rules, cross-section dependency graph, zero-invention + status-protocol application.\n- `references/input-signal-catalog.md` — what-to-look-for → which-context-pack-file mapping tables (source-code and documentation).\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `tech-blueprints` — **invoked by this skill in Step 0** (not a separate orchestration step): selects the technology-stack blueprints, writes `artifacts/inputs/tech-stack-selection.md`, and stages only the selected blueprints into `artifacts/inputs/tech-blueprints/`. That selection file is authoritative over the blueprint library — see the Tech-Blueprint Analysis section of `references/input-signal-catalog.md`. Callers pass `stack` through this skill; they do not run `tech-blueprints` separately.\n- `generate-context-pack` — downstream consumer: turns each `## For <file>` section into a normative context-pack file (with `when_to_read` routing frontmatter).\n- `transcribe-meetings` — upstream: produces `meeting-analysis.md` from MP4 transcripts, which this skill folds in when present.\n","isError":false}}
