{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_018UHgcenpDbHytPuNW3ib1V","result":"---\nname: tech-blueprints\ndescription: >\n  Selects the technology-stack blueprints that apply to a project and stages them\n  into artifacts/inputs/ for context-pack generation. Ships a curated library of\n  platform (L1), application-stack (L2), agentic (L3), and cross-cutting (L4)\n  architecture blueprints, each carrying a machine-readable Normative Profile.\n  Produces tech-stack-selection.md — the authoritative record of which stack was\n  chosen, what trade-offs were accepted, and which blueprint rules are overridden.\n  Activate when the task mentions \"tech stack\", \"select blueprints\", \"stack\n  selection\", \"which platform\", or precedes context-pack generation.\nmetadata:\n  author: aipods-team\n  version: 1.1.0\n  category: context-pack\n  tags: context-pack, blueprints, tech-stack, architecture, selection, agent-native\n---\n\n# Tech Blueprints\n\nShips the blueprint library in `blueprints/` and stages the subset that applies to a project\ninto `artifacts/inputs/`. **This skill selects and stages; it does not author blueprints and\ndoes not generate context-pack files.** Its output is the input contract for\n`analyze-context-pack-inputs`.\n\nThe library holds *comparative alternatives* carrying mutually exclusive mandates — one L1\nplatform requires CDK, another Terraform, another Helm. Downstream skills operate under a Zero\nInvention Policy and therefore have no authority to choose between them. **Selection is this\nskill's reason to exist.**\n\n## Generation shape & protocol route\n\n**Shape: list-shape** — one selection document plus N staged blueprint files, each written and\nflushed as it is produced. Apply `execution-protocol.md` in this order:\n\n`§2` (FIRST/LAST `_progress.json`, list-shape variant) → `§10.5` (tool discipline incl.\n§10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback — **blueprints contain `§` throughout, so the full-file-`Write`\npath applies to every staged file**) → `§11` (harness output sidecar). Also: `§5` (FIC\nmonitoring), `§7` (REPAIR when `failure_feedback` present), `§9` (non-interactive).\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Type | Required | Default | Description |\n|-----------|------|----------|---------|-------------|\n| `base_path` | string | No | `./` | Project root. All output paths derive from it. |\n| `selected_inputs` | string | No | — | Newline- or comma-separated list of paths under `OUTPUT_INPUTS` that may be read when deriving the selection. Passed through by `analyze-context-pack-inputs`. When absent, all of `OUTPUT_INPUTS` is in scope. |\n| `stack` | string | No | — | Comma-separated `blueprint_id` list (e.g. `aws,mobile-native,langgraph`). When supplied, selection is non-interactive. When omitted, derive from project inputs per Step 2. |\n| `failure_feedback` | string | No | — | REPAIR-mode directives (triggers §7). |\n\n**STOP-GATE — abort before any write only if:**\n- `stack` is omitted **and** `{base_path}/artifacts/inputs/` contains no project material at\n  all (brief, requirements, transcripts, source) from which to derive anything — write a Gap\n  Report and EXIT per §9. With material present this gate does not fire, however thin that\n  material is.\n\n**Not STOP-GATE conditions — resolve and continue:**\n- **No L1 platform derivable.** Select zero L1 per Selection rule 1 and carry on with L2/L3/L4.\n  A deliverable with no hosting surface has no platform to find; reporting that as a blocker\n  confuses \"there is no answer\" with \"I could not find the answer\".\n- **More than one L1 candidate.** Do not guess and do not abort. Pick none, record every\n  candidate under `## Open questions` in the selection document with what would decide between\n  them, and select the L2/L3/L4 blueprints — which are unaffected by the platform choice. The\n  downstream analyzer marks platform-dependent sections `pending`, which is a context pack with\n  a known hole in it. That beats no context pack, which is what aborting produces.\n\n**Never block the context pack on a blueprint question.** This skill is complementary: it\nsupplies defaults where the project material is silent. A missing or ambiguous *blueprint*\nanswer degrades the pack; it does not invalidate it. The only thing worth stopping for is an\nempty input set, because then there is genuinely nothing to describe.\n\n## Path resolution\n\n```\nLIBRARY       = {skill_dir}/blueprints/\nOUTPUT_INPUTS = {base_path}/artifacts/inputs/\nSELECTION     = {OUTPUT_INPUTS}/tech-stack-selection.md\nSTAGED        = {OUTPUT_INPUTS}/tech-blueprints/\nPROGRESS      = {base_path}/artifacts/outputs/tech-blueprint-selection/\n```\n\nCreate `STAGED` and `PROGRESS` if missing. Keep `_progress.json` in `PROGRESS`, never in\n`OUTPUT_INPUTS` — a stray file there becomes an analyzed input.\n\n## Session identity\n\nDerive `SESSION_ID` per `execution-protocol.md §1` with TYPE prefix `BLUEPRINT`. REPAIR reuses\nthe prior `SESSION_ID` from the existing audit filename.\n\n## Selection rules\n\n1. **At most one L1 — and zero is a valid answer.** L1 is the *cloud platform*, so it applies\n   only to a deliverable that is hosted somewhere. A standalone CLI, a library, a local script,\n   an offline utility, or anything else with no hosting or deployment surface has **no L1**,\n   and that is a correct selection, not a gap. Selecting zero L1 is never a reason to\n   STOP-GATE. See `blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md` for disqualifiers and the decision\n   matrix when there *is* a platform to choose.\n\n   When you select no L1, say so explicitly in the selection document\n   (`## Selected blueprints` → `L1: none — no hosting or deployment surface in scope`) so a\n   later reader can tell a deliberate omission from an unanswered question. The L2/L3/L4\n   selection stands on its own: `node-typescript` + `security-baseline` + `pre-ship-checklist`\n   is a complete, useful selection for a local TypeScript tool.\n2. **L4 is always selected.** `security-baseline` and `pre-ship-checklist` are\n   platform-neutral and apply to every project. Removing one requires a recorded exception in\n   the selection document.\n3. **Agentic projects require the foundation.** If any L3 blueprint is selected,\n   `agent-architecture-principles` is selected with it — the framework blueprints assume its\n   vocabulary and produce incoherent rules without it.\n4. **L2 is optional and may be plural.** `mobile-native` applies to any project shipping a\n   mobile binary, regardless of platform.\n5. **Integration projects require the governance blueprint.** If `mulesoft-anypoint` is\n   selected, `api-led-integration` is selected with it — the L2 blueprint carries runtime and\n   toolchain rules and assumes the layering vocabulary the L4 blueprint defines. Note also that\n   `mulesoft-anypoint` is an integration tier, not a backend: it composes with an L1 selection\n   rather than replacing one.\n6. **Never stage an unselected blueprint.** A blueprint present in `STAGED` is treated as\n   project input by the analyzer. Presence is selection.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2)\n\nWrite `_progress.json` (list-shape: `total`, `completed`, `items[]`) to `PROGRESS` with\n`\"session_id\": \"initializing\"` before any other write. This prevents orchestrator SIGINT.\n\n### Step 1 — Load the catalog\n\nRead `references/blueprint-catalog.md` — the `blueprint_id` → layer → thesis → best-fit lookup\ntable. **Do not read the blueprints themselves at this step.** The catalog exists so selection\ncosts one file read rather than sixteen.\n\n### Step 2 — Resolve the selection\n\nIf `stack` is supplied, parse it and skip to validation.\n\nOtherwise derive it from project material in `OUTPUT_INPUTS` (project brief, requirements,\ntranscripts, source manifests) — restricted to `selected_inputs` when the caller supplied it —\napplying `blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md`:\n- Resolve disqualifiers first — residency, offline-first mobile, platform-engineering capacity.\n  These eliminate candidates outright and are cheaper than capability comparison.\n- Then match the binding constraint to the §5 selection rows.\n- Record any signal you could not resolve; it becomes an open question, not a guess.\n\nValidate against **Selection rules** above. On violation, STOP-GATE per §9.\n\n### Step 3 — Write the selection document\n\n`Write` `SELECTION` using the structure of `blueprints/_tech-stack-selection.template.md`:\nselected blueprints with justification, binding constraint, accepted trade-offs, deviations,\nand project-specific resolutions.\n\n**Accepted trade-offs are mandatory, not optional.** For each selected blueprint, read its\n\"When Not to Choose\" section and record which items this project is knowingly accepting. This\nis the section that stops platform limitations being rediscovered later as defects.\n\n**Deviations override blueprints.** Any `§10` maxim or `§14` rule the project will not follow\nis recorded here with justification. The analyzer emits the deviation and drops the overridden\nrule.\n\n**Record input-stated specifics as deviations up front — do not leave them to be discovered.**\nBefore writing `SELECTION`, compare each selected blueprint's `§14 Normative Profile` against\nthe concrete facts the project material already states (runtime and language versions,\nbuild/test/lint commands, IaC tool, deploy target). Every point where the project contradicts\nthe profile is a deviation, and it belongs in this section with the input as its justification:\n\n```\n- `node-typescript` `runtime: node>=22` → project targets **Node 18 LTS**\n  (source: `artifacts/inputs/documentation/brief.md`, line 22)\n```\n\nThe project's stated fact wins; this section is only the record of that, not the decision.\nWriting it here means the override carries a justification and survives REPAIR, instead of\nbeing re-derived — possibly differently — by the analyzer on every run. When the material\nstates nothing on a topic, there is no deviation: the blueprint applies as written, which is\nthe whole point of selecting it.\n\nA deviation is **not** a licence to weaken a security-baseline control or adopt an EOL runtime\nsilently. Record those as deviations too, but add them to the selection document's open\nquestions so a human sees the risk before the pack is approved.\n\n### Step 4 — Stage the selected blueprints (Write-Flush-Forget)\n\nFor EACH selected `blueprint_id`, in layer order (L1 → L2 → L3 → L4):\n\n1. **Copy** `LIBRARY/{layer}/{id}.md` to `STAGED/{layer}/{id}.md` — full-file `Write`, never\n   `Bash`/`cp`/`sed` (§10.5). Blueprints contain non-ASCII (`§`), so §10.5.1's full-file path\n   applies unconditionally.\n2. **Verify** the file exists, is non-empty, and its `§14 Normative Profile` fenced YAML block\n   is present and parses.\n3. **Update** `_progress.json` (`completed += 1`, append to `items[]`).\n4. **Flush** the content from working memory before the next file.\n\nDo not stage `_template.md` or `_tech-stack-selection.template.md` — they are authoring aids,\nnot project input.\n\n### REPAIR mode (§7)\n\nWith a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block: parse which selections changed. Re-write `SELECTION`,\nstage newly-selected blueprints, **delete de-selected ones from `STAGED`** (a stale blueprint\nleft behind silently contributes rules), preserve unchanged files verbatim, and record the\ndelta in the audit. Do not re-stage the whole set.\n\n### LAST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2, §11)\n\nIn this strict order (§11.4):\n1. Verify `SELECTION` exists and every selected blueprint is present in `STAGED` — and that\n   `STAGED` contains **nothing else**.\n2. Flip `_progress.json` to `COMPLETED`.\n3. **Harness output sidecar (§11)** — apply when the prompt has a `## Run metadata` block with\n   `output_file`. Output parameters this skill produces:\n   - `tech_stack_selection_path`: path to `SELECTION`.\n   - `staged_blueprints_path`: path to `STAGED`.\n4. `final_response` — no tool calls after the sidecar write.\n\n## Error handling\n\n- Unknown `blueprint_id` in `stack` → STOP-GATE; list valid ids from the catalog in the Gap\n  Report. Never substitute the nearest match.\n- Two L1 ids → STOP-GATE naming both. This is not resolvable by inference.\n- L3 selected without `agent-architecture-principles` → add it automatically and note the\n  addition in the selection document.\n- `mulesoft-anypoint` selected without `api-led-integration` → add it automatically and note the\n  addition in the selection document.\n- Project material insufficient to derive a selection → Gap Report + EXIT. A guessed platform\n  produces a confidently wrong context pack, which is worse than none.\n\n## Reference files\n\n- `references/blueprint-catalog.md` — `blueprint_id` → layer, thesis, best-fit, anti-fit lookup. Read in Step 1.\n- `blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md` — decision matrix, disqualifiers, and common mis-selections. Read in Step 2.\n- `blueprints/_tech-stack-selection.template.md` — structure for the selection document. Read in Step 3.\n- `blueprints/_template.md` — the 14-section blueprint template. Only needed when authoring a new blueprint.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `analyze-context-pack-inputs` — downstream consumer. Reads `SELECTION` first, then extracts only from staged blueprints; its `references/input-signal-catalog.md` holds the section → context-pack-file routing.\n- `generate-context-pack` — turns that analysis into normative context-pack files.\n","isError":false}}
