{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01SmfLEoCRor6puarmuLQ9ZP","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/references/blueprint-catalog.md\" lines=\"184\" start=\"1\" end=\"184\" returned=\"184\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Blueprint Catalog\n     2|\n     3|## Context Contract\n     4|\n     5|- **H1 Title:** Blueprint Catalog\n     6|- **Inputs:** none — this is the lookup table Step 1 consults.\n     7|- **Outputs:** none — it defines, per blueprint, its `blueprint_id`, layer, path, thesis, and fit.\n     8|- **Carries Forward:** the selected `blueprint_id` set used by Step 3 and Step 4.\n     9|- **Flush After:** n/a (reference).\n    10|- **Used by step:** SKILL.md Step 1 and Step 2.\n    11|\n    12|Read this instead of the blueprints themselves when selecting. Selection costs one file read;\n    13|reading the library costs sixteen. Open an individual blueprint only when its \"When Not to\n    14|Choose\" section is needed for the Accepted trade-offs section of the selection document.\n    15|\n    16|Staged path is `artifacts/inputs/tech-blueprints/{layer_dir}/{blueprint_id}.md`.\n    17|\n    18|---\n    19|\n    20|## L1 — Cloud platforms (select EXACTLY ONE)\n    21|\n    22|`layer_dir: l1-clouds`\n    23|\n    24|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    25||---|---|---|---|\n    26|| `aws` | Maximum control and breadth; you assemble primitives rather than accept a platform's opinion, and pay for it in platform-engineering capacity. | Event-driven at scale · regulated workloads needing granular authorization · polyglot estates | Small teams optimizing time-to-market · no platform-engineering capacity · thin-backend web |\n    27|| `azure` | The enterprise identity and compliance cloud. | Corporate apps behind org identity · regulated enterprise · existing .NET/GitHub estates | **Offline-first mobile** · consumer time-to-market · no Microsoft estate |\n    28|| `gcp` | The data, AI, and real-time cloud, with the strongest client-side DX via its backend-as-a-service layer. | Real-time and offline-first mobile · data/AI-intensive · small teams | Enterprise MS/Oracle estates · needs broadest service catalog · single-incumbent procurement |\n    29|| `oracle` | Database-centric; the data tier is the architecture. | Database-intensive transactional · regulated finance/health/ERP · existing Oracle estate | Consumer web/mobile DX · no Oracle investment · iOS-heavy mobile programs |\n    30|| `ovh` | Sovereignty and cost predictability on open standards; no egress billing, no proprietary runtime. | European residency mandates · media/streaming-heavy · deliberate open-source assembly | No Kubernetes capability · needs managed BaaS out of the box · needs broad service catalog |\n    31|| `vercel` | A frontend cloud used as a backend-for-frontend. | React/Next.js web-primary · no infrastructure capacity · preview-per-PR matters | Long-running or stateful backends · data-gravity architectures · sovereignty mandates |\n    32|| `cloudflare` | Stateful serverless at the edge; compute and state co-located, single-writer correctness. | Real-time collaborative state · agent workloads needing per-entity persistence · egress-sensitive media | Relational/analytical workloads · broad managed catalog · conventional long-running runtimes |\n    33|\n    34|**Disqualifier shortcuts** (full matrix in `blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md §3`):\n    35|\n    36|| Constraint | Eliminates |\n    37||---|---|\n    38|| European sovereignty / strict residency | `vercel`; scrutinize US hyperscalers |\n    39|| Offline-first mobile is a requirement | `azure`, `vercel`, `oracle` |\n    40|| No platform-engineering capacity | `aws`, `oracle`, `ovh` |\n    41|| Long-running / stateful / connection-oriented backend | `vercel` |\n    42|| iOS builds cannot use an external macOS runner | `oracle`, `ovh` |\n    43|| Multi-cloud portability mandated | `vercel`; favors `ovh` |\n    44|\n    45|---\n    46|\n    47|## L2 — Application stacks (SELECT SEVERAL — composable by role)\n    48|\n    49|`layer_dir: l2-stacks`\n    50|\n    51|**L2 is not a single pick.** A typical project selects one **backend**, one **frontend**, and\n    52|`mobile-native` if it ships a binary. `react-web` + `java-spring` + `mobile-native` is a normal\n    53|selection. This layer is what supplies `coding-standards.md`, `test-standards.md`, and\n    54|`validation-tools.md` — the language-level rules and exact commands — so under-selecting here\n    55|produces a context pack with no coding standards.\n    56|\n    57|### Backend (select one; two only for a documented polyglot boundary)\n    58|\n    59|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    60||---|---|---|---|\n    61|| `java-spring` | Enterprise backend default: mature framework, strong typing, deepest operational tooling; ceremony and startup weight are the cost. | Complex transactional domains · long-lived enterprise systems · existing JVM investment | Serverless without native compilation · thin CRUD domains · no JVM experience |\n    62|| `dotnet` | Unified first-party platform — one vendor supplies language, runtime, web framework, ORM, tests, orchestration. | Microsoft estates · complex transactional domains · single coherent toolchain preferred | No C# experience · Node/Python-native surrounding ecosystem · in-process agents required |\n    63|| `node-typescript` | One language across client and server; the type contract spans the whole system. | I/O-bound APIs and BFFs · shared types across web/mobile/server · agentic workloads | CPU-bound work · decade-scale stability needs · heavyweight transactional semantics |\n    64|| `python` | The default for AI, data, and agentic workloads — every agent framework and ML library targets it first. | **Agentic and AI services** · data/ML pipelines · rapid iteration on I/O-bound APIs | CPU-bound without native extensions · large teams needing compiler-enforced boundaries |\n    65|\n    66|### Frontend (select one)\n    67|\n    68|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    69||---|---|---|---|\n    70|| `react-web` | Component-driven UI with server state and client state deliberately separated. | Interactive product surfaces · shared types with a TypeScript backend · design system across web and mobile | Content sites where a lighter framework suffices · no frontend capacity · SEO/payload dominate |\n    71|\n    72|### Client (select if shipping a mobile binary)\n    73|\n    74|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    75||---|---|---|---|\n    76|| `mobile-native` | Mobile is not \"a client that calls an API\"; build, signing, store review, and update cadence are architectural constraints. | **Any project shipping an iOS or Android binary**, on any L1 platform | Web-only products |\n    77|\n    78|### Full-stack delivery model (replaces a backend selection)\n    79|\n    80|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    81||---|---|---|---|\n    82|| `google-app-stack` | Framework-first delivery where the backend-as-a-service layer replaces most of the API tier. | Real-time/offline-first · small teams shipping web+mobile together · sync correctness over backend control | Complex server-side domain logic · strict relational cores · portability requirements |\n    83|| `supabase-postgres` | Postgres as the whole backend — auth, storage, realtime, and authorization are database features. **RLS is the entire security boundary.** | Small teams needing a full backend fast · Firebase velocity on relational data · **self-hostable, so the sovereignty case** | Complex server-side domain logic · teams unwilling to own RLS rigorously |\n    84|| `server-rendered-monolith` | One deployable, server-rendered HTML, one database, no API layer. Correct for more projects than this library otherwise admits. | CRUD-heavy business apps · small teams · web-only products · time-to-value dominates | Native mobile as a first-class client · highly interactive UI · components with different scaling profiles |\n    85|\n    86|### Deployment architecture (composes with a backend, not a replacement)\n    87|\n    88|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    89||---|---|---|---|\n    90|| `kubernetes-native` | Kubernetes as the portable substrate; the deployment target is a conformant cluster, not a cloud. | Multi-cloud or exit-optionality mandates · sovereignty · polyglot estates with many services | Small teams · few services · portability asserted rather than required |\n    91|\n    92|### Integration tier (composes with a backend and an L1; does not replace either)\n    93|\n    94|| blueprint_id | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n    95||---|---|---|---|\n    96|| `mulesoft-anypoint` | The integration tier as a governed product estate: every connection is a versioned, contract-first, policy-enforced API asset rather than point-to-point code. | Enterprise estates with many systems of record · existing Anypoint investment · SAP/Salesforce/mainframe-heavy landscapes · programmes where API reuse and governance are the deliverable | Fewer than ~12 integrations · no MuleSoft capability on the team · **exit-optionality mandates** · event streaming rather than request/response |\n    97|\n    98|**Selection rules for L2:**\n    99|- `google-app-stack` **replaces** a backend selection — it removes the API tier rather than\n   100|  implementing one. Do not pair it with `java-spring`, `dotnet`, `node-typescript`, or `python`\n   101|  without a documented boundary explaining which surface each owns.\n   102|- Select `python` or `node-typescript` when the project is agentic and agents must run\n   103|  in-process; `java-spring` and `dotnet` require an out-of-process agent (see their §7).\n   104|- `react-web` + `node-typescript` share types end to end; that pairing is the reason to choose\n   105|  both.\n   106|- `mulesoft-anypoint` is an **integration tier, not a backend**. It composes with an L1 platform\n   107|  (where the source systems live) and, where the project also builds application services, with a\n   108|  backend blueprint. Selecting it alone is correct for an integration-only engagement; selecting\n   109|  it *instead of* a backend for a project that also ships application code leaves that code\n   110|  without coding standards.\n   111|- Select `api-led-integration` (L4) whenever `mulesoft-anypoint` is selected. The L2 blueprint\n   112|  carries the runtime, toolchain and project-structure rules; the L4 blueprint carries the\n   113|  vendor-neutral layering, versioning and contract-governance rules that outlive the platform.\n   114|- Selecting **no** L2 backend leaves `coding-standards.md` and `validation-tools.md` with no\n   115|  source. That is a valid outcome only for an infrastructure-only engagement.\n   116|\n   117|---\n   118|\n   119|## L3 — Agentic (optional; `agent-architecture-principles` is required if any other L3 is selected)\n   120|\n   121|`layer_dir: l3-agentic`\n   122|\n   123|| blueprint_id | Axis | Thesis | Best fit | Anti-fit |\n   124||---|---|---|---|---|\n   125|| `agent-architecture-principles` | foundation | Agents are systems that decide their own control flow; everything difficult follows from that. | Open-ended tasks · tool use against changing state · non-enumerable decision procedures | Known fixed sequences · latency-critical paths · ungatable unrecoverable actions |\n   126|| `langgraph` | authoring | Agents as explicit state graphs; engineer owns topology, model owns routing within it. | Long-horizon agents needing resumption · human-in-the-loop approval · inspectable, regression-tested behavior | Simple single-loop tool use · minimal-abstraction preference · prototype speed priority |\n   127|| `google-agent-stack` | authoring + runtime | The most complete single-vendor stack, with an open interoperability protocol other vendors implement. | Existing GCP estates · multi-vendor agent landscapes · one-vendor authoring + runtime | No GCP footprint · framework-agnostic authoring standardization · mandated model-vendor neutrality |\n   128|| `vendor-agent-sdks` | authoring | The thinnest viable abstraction — the model vendor's own agent loop, no framework between your code and the model. | Single-agent tool loops · small dependency surface · already committed to one model vendor | Long-horizon agents needing durable resumption · model-vendor portability · complex conditional topologies |\n   129|| `crewai` | authoring | Role-based crews with delegated tasks. Fastest to a working multi-agent prototype; weakest on determinism and state. | Prototyping decomposition · content/research pipelines · role metaphor genuinely matches the work | **Production systems needing resumption or branching** · latency/cost-sensitive paths · single-agent problems |\n   130|| `aws-agent-stack` | authoring + runtime | Cleanest authoring/runtime separation: open-source SDK plus a framework-agnostic managed runtime of composable services. | Existing AWS estates · agents needing enterprise identity and session isolation · reversible framework choice | No AWS footprint · edge or on-device agents · want a single-vendor bundle |\n   131|| `microsoft-agent-stack` | authoring + runtime | Agents governed like corporate applications — corporate identity, API-gateway control, compliance posture. | Azure estates · corporate SSO and audited agent access · .NET in-process agents · regulated environments | No Microsoft estate · Python-first teams wanting the widest ecosystem · edge agents |\n   132|| `mcp-tool-integration` | **tool boundary** | MCP standardizes agent-to-tool the way HTTP standardized client-to-server; tools outlive framework decisions. | More than one agent · tools shared across teams · framework may change · exposing internal systems safely | One agent with three bespoke tools · latency budget cannot absorb a process hop |\n   133|| `agent-evals-observability` | **cross-cutting** | Agents are non-deterministic, so correctness is measured, not proved. Without an eval harness every prompt change is unfalsifiable. | **Any agent intended for production** · continuous prompt/model changes · consequential actions | Throwaway prototypes · deterministic workflows |\n   134|\n   135|**Selection heuristic** — the three-way authoring decision is SDK vs framework vs managed\n   136|runtime (`vendor-agent-sdks §2`). Start at `vendor-agent-sdks`; escalate to `langgraph` on\n   137|exactly three signals: durable resumption, conditional topology, or human-in-the-loop gating\n   138|becoming load-bearing. `crewai` is a prototyping choice — selecting it for production requires\n   139|a recorded migration decision.\n   140|\n   141|**Runtime follows the L1 platform**, not the framework: `aws-agent-stack` on AWS,\n   142|`microsoft-agent-stack` on Azure, `google-agent-stack` on GCP. All three host\n   143|third-party-authored agents, so authoring and runtime are separable choices.\n   144|\n   145|**Additionally select for any agentic project:**\n   146|- `agent-architecture-principles` — required whenever any other L3 blueprint is selected.\n   147|- `mcp-tool-integration` — whenever tools are exposed to agents (nearly always).\n   148|- `agent-evals-observability` — whenever the agent reaches production.\n   149|\n   150|---\n   151|\n   152|## L4 — Cross-cutting (ALWAYS selected)\n   153|\n   154|`layer_dir: l4-concerns`\n   155|\n   156|| blueprint_id | Always? | Covers |\n   157||---|---|---|\n   158|| `security-baseline` | **Yes** | Platform-neutral security invariants: identity delegation, data-tier authorization, input handling, secrets, abuse control, supply chain, agent-specific controls. Each L1 blueprint's §8 states how that platform satisfies them. |\n   159|| `pre-ship-checklist` | **Yes** | Verification gates: CI blocking checks, auth failure paths, assisted review passes, operational readiness, legal/data, final scan. Feeds `test-standards.md` and `validation-tools.md`. |\n   160|| `observability-opentelemetry` | Recommended | Instrument once against an open standard, export anywhere. Makes the six divergent §9 vocabularies comparable and removes vendor SDKs from application code. Select for any multi-service estate. |\n   161|| `iac-platform-engineering` | Recommended | The shared discipline behind every blueprint's \"no manual changes\" rule: state segmentation, module structure, plan-as-review-artifact, policy gates, mandatory tagging. Select whenever there is infrastructure to define. |\n   162|| `data-platform-rag` | If retrieval | Retrieval quality, not model quality, decides whether a grounded system works — and retrieval is a security boundary. Select for any RAG or knowledge-grounded feature. |\n   163|| `api-led-integration` | If integration | Vendor-neutral layering (System/Process/Experience), contract-first delivery, version immutability, canonical models, shared error contracts, degraded-operation design, and reuse governance. Select for any project integrating three or more systems of record or exposing APIs consumed by other teams. Required alongside `mulesoft-anypoint`. |\n   164|\n   165|`security-baseline` and `pre-ship-checklist` are always selected; removing either requires a\n   166|recorded exception. The other four are conditional on the project having multiple services,\n   167|infrastructure, retrieval, or a multi-system integration estate respectively.\n   168|\n   169|---\n   170|\n   171|## Not in the library\n   172|\n   173|`domain.md` and `customer-background.md` in the generated context pack derive from project and\n   174|customer inputs — brief, requirements, transcripts, source code. No blueprint produces them.\n   175|A blueprint-only input set yields `[TO BE COMPLETED]` skeletons for those two by design.\n   176|\n   177|## Authoring aids (never staged)\n   178|\n   179|| File | Purpose |\n   180||---|---|\n   181|| `blueprints/_template.md` | The 14-section blueprint template, for authoring a new blueprint. |\n   182|| `blueprints/_tech-stack-selection.template.md` | Structure for the selection document (Step 3). |\n   183|| `blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md` | Decision matrix, disqualifiers, common mis-selections (Step 2). |\n   184|\n</file>","isError":false}}
