{"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_013pozeVj7UBQ39daGdydrU7","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md","offset":1,"limit":150}}}
{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_013pozeVj7UBQ39daGdydrU7","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/blueprints/00-choosing-a-platform.md\" lines=\"171\" start=\"1\" end=\"150\" returned=\"150\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Choosing a Platform\n     2|\n     3|> **Purpose** — The decision layer over the blueprint library. Read this before reading any\n     4|> blueprint; read it again before overriding one.\n     5|> **Scope** — L1 cloud platforms and L2 application stacks. Agent framework and runtime\n     6|> selection is covered in `l3-agentic/agent-architecture-principles.md`.\n     7|\n     8|---\n     9|\n    10|## 1. How to use this document\n    11|\n    12|The library contains parallel blueprints that each argue for their own platform. That is\n    13|correct for authoring and useless for deciding. This document is the chooser.\n    14|\n    15|**Selection is a required step, not an optional one.** The downstream context-pack pipeline\n    16|cannot resolve conflicts between blueprints — it has no authority to choose between one\n    17|platform's mandate and another's. Feeding it the whole library produces contradictory rules.\n    18|Select first, then place only the selected blueprints in the analysis input set.\n    19|\n    20|## 2. The three questions that decide most projects\n    21|\n    22|Answer these before comparing feature matrices. In practice they settle the platform choice\n    23|more often than any capability comparison.\n    24|\n    25|**1. Where must the data live, and who must be able to compel access to it?**\n    26|A hard European residency or sovereignty mandate eliminates most of the library immediately.\n    27|This question is disqualifying, not weighting — answer it first.\n    28|\n    29|**2. Who will operate the platform on an ordinary Tuesday?**\n    30|Not who will build it. Platform-engineering capacity is the most commonly overestimated input\n    31|in this decision. A team without it should not select a platform whose advantage is breadth.\n    32|\n    33|**3. Is offline-first mobile a product requirement?**\n    34|This single requirement separates the library cleanly and is frequently discovered late, after\n    35|it is expensive.\n    36|\n    37|## 3. Disqualifiers\n    38|\n    39|Check these before evaluating fit. Each eliminates candidates outright.\n    40|\n    41|| Constraint | Eliminates |\n    42||---|---|\n    43|| European sovereignty / strict residency mandate | Vercel; scrutinize all US hyperscalers |\n    44|| Offline-first mobile is a core requirement | Azure, Vercel, Oracle (all require building it) |\n    45|| No platform-engineering capacity on the team | AWS, Oracle, OVH |\n    46|| Long-running, stateful, or connection-oriented backend | Vercel |\n    47|| iOS builds cannot depend on an external macOS runner | Oracle, OVH |\n    48|| Multi-cloud portability is mandated | Vercel; favors OVH and Kubernetes-native |\n    49|| No managed-service catalog breadth required, cost predictability dominates | favors OVH |\n    50|\n    51|## 4. Comparison\n    52|\n    53|| | AWS | Azure | GCP | Oracle | OVH | Vercel |\n    54||---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    55|| **Thesis** | Control and breadth | Enterprise identity | Data, AI, real-time | Database-centric | Sovereignty, cost | Frontend cloud |\n    56|| **Time to first production surface** | Slow | Moderate | Fast | Slow | Slow | Fastest |\n    57|| **Platform-engineering demand** | High | Moderate | Low–moderate | High | Highest | Minimal |\n    58|| **Offline-first mobile** | Good (managed GraphQL) | **Absent** | **Best in library** | Build it | Build it (self-hosted BaaS) | **Absent** |\n    59|| **Mobile CI (incl. iOS)** | First-party device farm | Hosted runners | Most complete first-party | **External runner required** | **External runner required** | External service required |\n    60|| **Managed agent runtime** | **Strongest separation of authoring/runtime** | Yes | **Strongest interop (A2A, MCP)** | None | None | None |\n    61|| **Identity** | Managed | **Strongest** | Managed | Managed | Self-hosted | **None — third party** |\n    62|| **Data residency control** | Broad, incl. gov | Broad, incl. sovereign | Broad | **Incl. dedicated regions** | **European by design** | Weak |\n    63|| **Cost shape** | Request + capacity | Provisioned throughput | Read volume | Capacity, predictable | Capacity, **no egress fees** | Invocation + third-party data |\n    64|| **Exit cost** | High | Moderate | Low compute / high BaaS | Low compute / high data | **Lowest** | Moderate, asymmetric |\n    65|\n    66|## 5. Selection by dominant constraint\n    67|\n    68|Read the row that matches the project's binding constraint.\n    69|\n    70|* **Regulatory compliance and corporate SSO dominate** → **Azure**. Accept that offline-first\n    71|  mobile must be built by hand or descoped.\n    72|* **European sovereignty and predictable cost dominate** → **OVHcloud**. Budget explicitly for\n    73|  operating a self-hosted backend-as-a-service and identity provider.\n    74|* **Real-time or offline-first mobile is the product** → **GCP**. Adopt the client SDK layer\n    75|  fully; partial adoption forfeits the reason to choose it.\n    76|* **The transactional database is the architecture** → **Oracle Cloud**. Especially where an\n    77|  Oracle estate already exists and retrieval must run against corporate data in place.\n    78|* **Scale, event-driven architecture, and granular authorization dominate** → **AWS**.\n    79|  Requires genuine platform-engineering capacity.\n    80|* **Web is the product and speed dominates** → **Vercel**. Treat the data tier as a\n    81|  third-party dependency from day one.\n    82|* **Portability is mandated above all** → **OVHcloud** or the L2 Kubernetes-native stack on any\n    83|  provider.\n    84|\n    85|## 6. Common mis-selections\n    86|\n    87|These are the failure patterns worth naming, because each is chosen for a reason that turns\n    88|out not to be the binding constraint.\n    89|\n    90|1. **Choosing AWS for breadth the project never uses.** The catalog is an advantage only when\n    91|   something in it is needed. Otherwise it is configuration surface.\n    92|2. **Choosing Azure without a Microsoft estate.** The identity and tooling cohesion that\n    93|   justifies Azure does not exist in isolation, and the mobile sync gap remains.\n    94|3. **Choosing GCP and then refusing the backend-as-a-service layer.** This purchases the\n    95|   platform's costs without its benefits.\n    96|4. **Choosing Vercel for a data-heavy system of record.** The compute model is request-scoped\n    97|   and the data tier is brokered; both are structural.\n    98|5. **Choosing OVHcloud without staffing the Kubernetes and self-hosted-component operating\n    99|   burden.** The cost advantage is real and the operating cost is also real.\n   100|6. **Deferring the offline-first question.** Discovering it after platform selection is the\n   101|   most expensive sequencing error in this library.\n   102|7. **Adopting an integration platform without its layering discipline.** Buying MuleSoft and\n   103|   then building point-to-point connections through it produces the original estate at higher\n   104|   licence cost. The reuse case is the layering, not the runtime.\n   105|\n   106|## 7. Layer interaction\n   107|\n   108|L1 and L2 are chosen together, not in sequence. Three combinations are worth naming:\n   109|\n   110|* **L1 GCP + L2 Google application stack** — the L1 blueprint covers infrastructure; the L2\n   111|  blueprint covers the framework-first delivery model. Most GCP projects need both.\n   112|* **L1 OVHcloud + L2 Supabase / Postgres-first** — §10.2 of the OVH blueprint mandates a\n   113|  self-hosted backend-as-a-service; the L2 blueprint is where that component is specified.\n   114|* **L1 any + L2 Kubernetes-native** — where portability is the binding constraint, the L2\n   115|  stack carries the architecture and L1 becomes a commodity capacity decision.\n   116|* **L1 any + L2 MuleSoft/Anypoint + L4 API-Led integration** — the integration tier is a\n   117|  separate concern from the platform hosting the source systems. MuleSoft does **not** replace an\n   118|  L1 selection: the systems being integrated still run somewhere, and that somewhere is the L1\n   119|  decision. The L2 blueprint carries the runtime and toolchain; the L4 blueprint carries the\n   120|  layering and contract-governance rules, which survive a change of integration vendor.\n   121|\n   122|---\n   123|\n   124|## 14. Normative Profile\n   125|\n   126|```yaml\n   127|blueprint_id: choosing-a-platform\n   128|blueprint_version: 1.0\n   129|layer: L4\n   130|\n   131|standards:\n   132|  - area: arch\n   133|    rule: \"Select the platform before generating a context pack; place only the selected blueprints in the analysis input set.\"\n   134|    evidence: \"§1\"\n   135|  - area: arch\n   136|    rule: \"Resolve data residency and sovereignty constraints before evaluating platform capability; they are disqualifying, not weighting.\"\n   137|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   138|  - area: arch\n   139|    rule: \"Determine whether offline-first mobile is a product requirement before platform selection.\"\n   140|    evidence: \"§2, §6.6\"\n   141|  - area: arch\n   142|    rule: \"When GCP is selected, adopt the Firebase client SDK layer fully; partial adoption forfeits the platform's advantage.\"\n   143|    evidence: \"§6.3\"\n   144|  - area: arch\n   145|    rule: \"When OVHcloud is selected, staff and budget the operation of a self-hosted backend-as-a-service and identity provider explicitly.\"\n   146|    evidence: \"§5, §6.5\"\n   147|  - area: arch\n   148|    rule: \"When Vercel is selected, treat the marketplace data tier as a third-party architectural dependency from the outset.\"\n   149|    evidence: \"§5, §6.4\"\n   150|  - area: arch\n</file>","isError":false}}
