{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01NPzKrK1XJ88iwwiquNszA5","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/blueprints/l2-stacks/node-typescript.md\" lines=\"316\" start=\"1\" end=\"315\" returned=\"315\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Architectural Blueprint: Node.js / TypeScript (Backend)\n     2|\n     3|> **Thesis** — One language across client and server: the type contract spans the whole system,\n     4|> and the cost is a runtime with weak CPU parallelism and an ecosystem that shifts underfoot.\n     5|> **Best fit** — I/O-bound APIs and BFFs · teams sharing types between web, mobile, and server ·\n     6|> agentic workloads, where the framework ecosystem is native.\n     7|> **Anti-fit** — CPU-bound workloads · long-lived enterprise systems needing decade-scale\n     8|> stability · domains requiring heavyweight transactional guarantees.\n     9|> **Layer** — L2 backend. Composes with any L1 platform; pairs naturally with `react-web`.\n    10|\n    11|---\n    12|\n    13|## 1. Executive Summary\n    14|\n    15|The reason to choose this stack is end-to-end type safety. A schema defined once flows into the\n    16|server, the web client, and the mobile client, and a breaking change fails the build rather\n    17|than production. That property is worth more than raw runtime performance for most product\n    18|work.\n    19|\n    20|The honest costs: the runtime is single-threaded for CPU work, the ecosystem churns faster than\n    21|any other stack here, and dependency-tree depth makes supply-chain review a standing obligation\n    22|rather than a one-time task.\n    23|\n    24|## 2. Language, Runtime, and Framework Baseline\n    25|\n    26|* **Language:** TypeScript in strict mode. Strict mode is not negotiable — the stack's entire\n    27|  value proposition is the type system, and a non-strict configuration forfeits most of it.\n    28|* **Runtime:** Node.js on an active LTS release. Alternative runtimes are viable where their\n    29|  constraints are understood, but Node remains the compatibility baseline.\n    30|* **Framework:** Fastify or Nest depending on the domain. Nest supplies dependency injection,\n    31|  module boundaries, and convention — worth its weight on large domains and a tax on small\n    32|  ones. Fastify is the right default for focused services.\n    33|* **Package manager:** pnpm with workspaces, for strict dependency resolution and monorepo\n    34|  support.\n    35|\n    36|## 3. Application Architecture and Layering\n    37|\n    38|* **Layering:** Route → service → repository, with domain logic independent of the HTTP\n    39|  framework so it is testable without a server.\n    40|* **Validation at the boundary.** A schema validator parses every inbound payload and derives\n    41|  the TypeScript type from the schema — never the reverse. Types are erased at runtime;\n    42|  validation is what actually protects the system.\n    43|* **Async and concurrency:** Everything is async by default. CPU-bound work belongs in worker\n    44|  threads or a different service — a synchronous loop blocks the event loop and stalls every\n    45|  concurrent request, which presents as total unresponsiveness rather than slowness.\n    46|* **Error handling:** A typed error hierarchy mapped centrally to responses. Unhandled promise\n    47|  rejections must crash the process rather than being suppressed; a process in an unknown state\n    48|  is more dangerous than a restarted one.\n    49|\n    50|## 4. Data Access and Persistence\n    51|\n    52|* **ORM / query builder:** A type-safe query builder or ORM where schema types are generated\n    53|  from the database schema and flow into application code.\n    54|* **Migrations:** Versioned, forward-only, checked in, applied as a deliberate step.\n    55|* **Connection pooling is the recurring failure on serverless L1 platforms.** Request-scoped\n    56|  compute multiplies connections until the database ceiling is hit. Use a pooler, and size it\n    57|  against the database, not the application.\n    58|* **Transactions:** Explicit and scoped to the use case; never spanning an HTTP call to another\n    59|  service.\n    60|\n    61|## 5. Project Structure and Code Organization\n    62|\n    63|```\n    64|apps/api/src/\n    65|  modules/<feature>/    routes, service, repository, schemas\n    66|  shared/               cross-cutting utilities\n    67|packages/\n    68|  core/                 domain logic and validators shared with clients\n    69|  db/                   schema and migrations\n    70|```\n    71|\n    72|* **Package by feature, not by technical layer.** Top-level `controllers/`, `services/`,\n    73|  `utils/` directories obscure boundaries as the codebase grows.\n    74|* **Naming:** `camelCase` for values and functions, `PascalCase` for types and classes,\n    75|  `kebab-case` for filenames. One exported concern per file where practical.\n    76|* **`any` is prohibited.** Use `unknown` and narrow. An `any` in the diff is a defect, not a\n    77|  style preference — it silently disables checking for everything downstream.\n    78|* **No default exports** — they defeat rename refactoring and make imports inconsistent.\n    79|* **Logging:** Structured JSON via a fast logger, with redaction configured for sensitive\n    80|  fields. `console.log` is prohibited outside scripts.\n    81|\n    82|## 6. Build, Test, and CI\n    83|\n    84|* **Build:** TypeScript compilation or a bundler, with a task-graph runner in monorepos so only\n    85|  changed packages rebuild.\n    86|* **Test:** Vitest or the runtime's built-in test runner for unit tests; Supertest or the\n    87|  framework's injection API for HTTP-level tests; Testcontainers for real database engines.\n    88|* **Type checking is a separate CI gate from the build.** A bundler that strips types without\n    89|  checking them will happily ship type errors.\n    90|* **Lint and format** enforced in CI, never reviewed by humans.\n    91|\n    92|## 7. AI and Agent Integration\n    93|\n    94|This is the stack's strongest differentiator relative to the JVM and .NET. The major agent\n    95|frameworks and both vendor agent SDKs ship first-class TypeScript support, MCP servers are\n    96|straightforward to author here, and the same validators used for HTTP boundaries define tool\n    97|schemas. Agentic control flow can run **in-process** alongside domain logic rather than behind\n    98|an HTTP hop.\n    99|\n   100|Where the project is agentic, this is a significant argument for the stack — see the L3\n   101|blueprints for framework selection.\n   102|\n   103|## 8. Security\n   104|\n   105|Inherits the L4 baseline. Stack specifics:\n   106|\n   107|* **Dependency depth is the primary risk.** The transitive graph is deeper than any other stack\n   108|  here. Audit in CI, pin versions, and review what AI assistants add.\n   109|* Validate and narrow every external input at the boundary; a parsed schema is the trust\n   110|  boundary.\n   111|* Never interpolate into SQL; use the query builder's parameterization.\n   112|* Prototype-pollution-prone patterns (deep merges of untrusted objects) are prohibited.\n   113|* Set security headers explicitly through middleware.\n   114|\n   115|## 9. Observability\n   116|\n   117|OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation covers the common frameworks and database drivers with\n   118|minimal wiring. Structured JSON logs with a correlation identifier propagated through async\n   119|context. Event-loop lag is a first-class metric on this stack and should be alerted on — it is\n   120|the leading indicator of the blocking failure described in §3.\n   121|\n   122|## 10. Playbook\n   123|\n   124|1. **TypeScript strict mode, and `any` is prohibited.** The type system is the reason to be\n   125|   here; weakening it forfeits the choice.\n   126|2. **Validate at the boundary and derive types from schemas.** Types vanish at runtime;\n   127|   validators do not.\n   128|3. **Never block the event loop.** CPU-bound work goes to a worker thread or another service.\n   129|4. **Pool database connections against the database ceiling,** especially on request-scoped\n   130|   serverless compute.\n   131|5. **Treat the dependency tree as an attack surface.** Audit in CI, pin, and review additions.\n   132|\n   133|## 11. Cost and Performance Posture\n   134|\n   135|* **What scales the cost:** Instance count under concurrent I/O. Memory per instance is low,\n   136|  which makes this stack cheap to scale horizontally.\n   137|* **The trap:** A single synchronous CPU-bound operation stalling all concurrent requests. It\n   138|  looks like an outage, not a slowdown, and load tests that measure throughput alone miss it.\n   139|* **Controls:** Event-loop-lag alerting, worker threads for CPU work, connection pooling.\n   140|\n   141|## 12. Environments and Configuration\n   142|\n   143|Environment variables validated through a schema at startup, so a missing or malformed value\n   144|fails immediately rather than at first use. Never read raw `process.env` outside that module.\n   145|Secrets from the platform secret store.\n   146|\n   147|## 13. Compliance and Exit Path\n   148|\n   149|* **Exit cost:** Low. TypeScript and the frameworks are open source and portable across every\n   150|  L1 platform in this library. The real coupling is ecosystem churn — the maintenance cost is\n   151|  ongoing rather than at exit.\n   152|\n   153|---\n   154|\n   155|## When Not to Choose Node.js / TypeScript\n   156|\n   157|1. The workload is CPU-bound — image, video, or numerical processing belongs elsewhere.\n   158|2. The system must remain stable for a decade with minimal maintenance; ecosystem churn makes\n   159|   that expensive.\n   160|3. The domain needs heavyweight transactional semantics that the JVM and .NET ecosystems\n   161|   support more maturely.\n   162|\n   163|---\n   164|\n   165|## 14. Normative Profile\n   166|\n   167|```yaml\n   168|blueprint_id: node-typescript\n   169|blueprint_version: 1.0\n   170|layer: L2\n   171|role: backend\n   172|\n   173|tech_signals:\n   174|  - signal: \"TypeScript in strict mode\"\n   175|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   176|  - signal: \"Node.js on an active LTS release\"\n   177|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   178|  - signal: \"Fastify for focused services, or NestJS for large domains needing DI and module boundaries\"\n   179|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   180|  - signal: \"pnpm with workspaces for dependency resolution and monorepo support\"\n   181|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   182|  - signal: \"Zod or an equivalent schema validator at every boundary, with types derived from schemas\"\n   183|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   184|  - signal: \"Drizzle or Prisma with schema types generated from the database\"\n   185|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   186|  - signal: \"Vitest for unit tests; Testcontainers for real database engines\"\n   187|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   188|  - signal: \"Pino or an equivalent structured JSON logger with field redaction\"\n   189|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   190|  - signal: \"OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation\"\n   191|    evidence: \"§9\"\n   192|  - signal: \"First-class TypeScript support in L3 agent frameworks and vendor agent SDKs; MCP servers authored in-stack\"\n   193|    evidence: \"§7\"\n   194|\n   195|standards:\n   196|  - area: coding\n   197|    rule: \"Enable TypeScript strict mode; non-strict configuration is prohibited.\"\n   198|    evidence: \"§2, §10.1\"\n   199|  - area: coding\n   200|    rule: \"The any type is prohibited; use unknown and narrow. An any in a diff is a defect, not a style preference.\"\n   201|    evidence: \"§5, §10.1\"\n   202|  - area: coding\n   203|    rule: \"Derive TypeScript types from validation schemas, never define schemas from types.\"\n   204|    evidence: \"§3, §10.2\"\n   205|  - area: coding\n   206|    rule: \"Package by feature; top-level technical-layer directories (controllers/, services/, utils/) are prohibited.\"\n   207|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   208|  - area: coding\n   209|    rule: \"Values and functions are camelCase, types and classes PascalCase, filenames kebab-case.\"\n   210|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   211|  - area: coding\n   212|    rule: \"Default exports are prohibited; they defeat rename refactoring and make imports inconsistent.\"\n   213|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   214|  - area: coding\n   215|    rule: \"Use a structured JSON logger with redaction; console.log outside scripts is prohibited.\"\n   216|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   217|  - area: coding\n   218|    rule: \"Read environment variables only through a startup-validated schema module; raw process.env access elsewhere is prohibited.\"\n   219|    evidence: \"§12\"\n   220|  - area: arch\n   221|    rule: \"Validate and parse every inbound payload at the boundary; the parsed schema is the trust boundary.\"\n   222|    evidence: \"§3, §10.2\"\n   223|  - area: arch\n   224|    rule: \"Never block the event loop; CPU-bound work belongs in a worker thread or a separate service.\"\n   225|    evidence: \"§3, §10.3\"\n   226|  - area: arch\n   227|    rule: \"Let unhandled promise rejections crash the process; suppressing them is prohibited.\"\n   228|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   229|  - area: arch\n   230|    rule: \"Keep domain logic independent of the HTTP framework so it is testable without a server.\"\n   231|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   232|  - area: arch\n   233|    rule: \"Scope transactions to a use case; a transaction must never span an HTTP call to another service.\"\n   234|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   235|  - area: arch\n   236|    rule: \"Pool database connections and size the pool against the database ceiling, especially on request-scoped serverless compute.\"\n   237|    evidence: \"§4, §10.4\"\n   238|  - area: arch\n   239|    rule: \"Map errors through a typed error hierarchy to responses centrally.\"\n   240|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   241|  - area: test\n   242|    rule: \"Run type checking as a CI gate separate from the build; a bundler that strips types without checking them will ship type errors.\"\n   243|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   244|  - area: test\n   245|    rule: \"Test persistence against the real database engine using Testcontainers.\"\n   246|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   247|  - area: test\n   248|    rule: \"Load tests must exercise concurrency, not throughput alone, to surface event-loop blocking.\"\n   249|    evidence: \"§11\"\n   250|  - area: security\n   251|    rule: \"Audit the dependency tree in CI, pin versions, and review dependencies added by AI assistants; transitive depth is this stack's primary security risk.\"\n   252|    evidence: \"§8, §10.5\"\n   253|  - area: security\n   254|    rule: \"Use query-builder parameterization; SQL string interpolation is prohibited.\"\n   255|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   256|  - area: security\n   257|    rule: \"Deep merges of untrusted objects are prohibited (prototype pollution).\"\n   258|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   259|  - area: security\n   260|    rule: \"Set security headers explicitly through middleware.\"\n   261|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   262|  - area: automation\n   263|    rule: \"Enforce lint and format in CI; style is never reviewed by humans.\"\n   264|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   265|  - area: automation\n   266|    rule: \"Use a task-graph runner in monorepos so only changed packages rebuild.\"\n   267|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   268|  - area: automation\n   269|    rule: \"Alert on event-loop lag as a leading indicator of blocking failures.\"\n   270|    evidence: \"§9, §11\"\n   271|\n   272|constraints:\n   273|  - constraint: \"Migrations are versioned, forward-only, checked in, and applied as a deliberate step.\"\n   274|    source: \"§4\"\n   275|  - constraint: \"Secrets come from the platform secret store, never from committed files.\"\n   276|    source: \"§12\"\n   277|  - constraint: \"CPU-bound workloads are out of scope for this stack.\"\n   278|    source: \"§11, When Not to Choose\"\n   279|\n   280|infra:\n   281|  - item: \"Connection pooling\"\n   282|    detail: \"External pooler required on request-scoped serverless compute; sized against the database ceiling.\"\n   283|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   284|  - item: \"Observability\"\n   285|    detail: \"OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation; structured JSON logs with correlation id through async context; event-loop lag as a first-class metric.\"\n   286|    evidence: \"§9\"\n   287|  - item: \"Agent hosting\"\n   288|    detail: \"Agentic control flow can run in-process alongside domain logic; no HTTP hop required.\"\n   289|    evidence: \"§7\"\n   290|\n   291|commands:\n   292|  - purpose: build\n   293|    command: \"pnpm build\"\n   294|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   295|  - purpose: typecheck\n   296|    command: \"pnpm tsc --noEmit\"\n   297|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   298|  - purpose: test\n   299|    command: \"pnpm vitest run\"\n   300|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   301|  - purpose: lint\n   302|    command: \"pnpm lint\"\n   303|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   304|  - purpose: security\n   305|    command: \"pnpm audit --audit-level=high\"\n   306|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   307|\n   308|open_questions:\n   309|  - question: \"Fastify or NestJS?\"\n   310|    why: \"Determined by domain size; Nest's conventions are a tax on small services.\"\n   311|  - question: \"Which ORM or query builder?\"\n   312|    why: \"Team preference; the requirement is that schema types are generated from the database.\"\n   313|  - question: \"Is an external connection pooler required?\"\n   314|    why: \"Determined by whether the L1 compute tier is request-scoped.\"\n   315|```\n</file>","isError":false}}
