{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01NiHMgMM1D8Q4Dp7MVVSmVM","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/blueprints/l4-concerns/security-baseline.md\" lines=\"251\" start=\"1\" end=\"250\" returned=\"250\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Cross-Cutting Concern: Security Baseline\n     2|\n     3|> **Thesis** — The security invariants every project must satisfy, stated once, independent\n     4|> of platform. Each blueprint's §8 states how *that* platform satisfies them.\n     5|> **Applies to** — every L1, L2, and L3 blueprint.\n     6|> **Does not cover** — verification and pre-ship gates; see `pre-ship-checklist.md`.\n     7|\n     8|---\n     9|\n    10|## 1. Executive Summary\n    11|\n    12|Six blueprints describe six different ways to satisfy the same small set of security\n    13|invariants. Those invariants do not vary by cloud, and repeating them per platform invites\n    14|drift. This document states them once as normative rules; the per-platform §8 sections state\n    15|the mechanism. Neither replaces the other: an invariant without a mechanism is unenforceable,\n    16|and a mechanism without an invariant is unjustified.\n    17|\n    18|The organizing principle: **security is a property of the shipping ritual, not a release\n    19|milestone.** Controls that are not wired into CI or infrastructure-as-code do not survive\n    20|contact with delivery pressure.\n    21|\n    22|## 2. Trust Boundaries\n    23|\n    24|Every architecture in this library has the same four boundaries. Name them explicitly before\n    25|designing controls.\n    26|\n    27|* **Client → Edge.** The mobile binary and the web bundle are public. Anything shipped to\n    28|  them is disclosed, including anything in an environment variable that reaches the client\n    29|  build. Treat the client as an untrusted input generator, never as an enforcement point.\n    30|* **Edge → Application.** The WAF and CDN tier. Volumetric and signature-based defense lives\n    31|  here, not in application code.\n    32|* **Application → Data.** Authorization decisions live here or in the data tier. This is the\n    33|  boundary most often left implicit, and the one that produces the worst failures.\n    34|* **Application → Third party.** Model providers, payment processors, and analytics. Every\n    35|  crossing is a data-disclosure decision and a spend decision simultaneously.\n    36|\n    37|## 3. Identity and Authorization\n    38|\n    39|* **Mandatory:** Delegate authentication to a managed identity provider. Do not implement\n    40|  password storage, reset flows, session issuance, or MFA in application code.\n    41|* **Mandatory:** Authorization is enforced server-side or in the data tier. Client-side role\n    42|  checks are presentation logic and carry no security weight.\n    43|* **Mandatory:** Where the data tier supports row-level authorization, enable it on every\n    44|  table holding user data — not only the tables currently queried from the client. A table\n    45|  without a policy is readable by anyone holding the public key.\n    46|* **Mandatory:** Test authorization policies by attempting access as a different user, and as\n    47|  an unauthenticated caller. An untested policy is a false-confidence generator.\n    48|* **Recommended:** Externalize authorization rules from application code once they exceed\n    49|  simple ownership checks.\n    50|\n    51|## 4. Input Handling and Output Safety\n    52|\n    53|* **Mandatory:** Validate and sanitize every input server-side. Schema validation in the\n    54|  client is a user-experience feature and provides no protection.\n    55|* **Mandatory:** Validate type, length, range, and format — not only shape.\n    56|* **Mandatory:** Sanitize error responses. Stack traces, table names, column names, query\n    57|  fragments, and internal hostnames must never reach a client. Log detail server-side; return\n    58|  a generic message and a correlation identifier.\n    59|* **Restriction:** Never interpolate untrusted input into a query, a shell command, a\n    60|  template, or a model prompt without an explicit escaping or parameterization step.\n    61|\n    62|## 5. Secrets\n    63|\n    64|* **Mandatory:** Publishable keys may ship to clients. Secret keys — service-role, payment\n    65|  secret, model-provider keys — remain server-side, injected from a managed secret store.\n    66|* **Mandatory:** No secret is committed to version control, at any point in history.\n    67|* **Mandatory:** A key suspected of exposure is rotated immediately. Assessment does not\n    68|  precede rotation.\n    69|* **Mandatory:** Model-provider credentials never reach a mobile binary or web bundle. Client\n    70|  AI calls route through a server-side proxy that holds the credential and applies quota.\n    71|* **Recommended:** Prefer workload identity federation over static keys for service-to-service\n    72|  authentication wherever the platform supports it.\n    73|\n    74|## 6. Perimeter and Abuse Control\n    75|\n    76|* **Mandatory:** Rate-limit every endpoint that reaches a metered third-party API. An\n    77|  unthrottled model endpoint is an unbounded liability, not merely a performance risk.\n    78|* **Mandatory:** Set hard spend caps and alert thresholds on every metered provider, with an\n    79|  alert well below the cap. See §11 of each blueprint for where the control lives.\n    80|* **Mandatory:** Restrict CORS to known origins in production.\n    81|* **Mandatory:** Place a WAF with managed rule sets in front of every public entry point.\n    82|* **Recommended:** Apply a challenge (CAPTCHA or equivalent) to unauthenticated public forms.\n    83|\n    84|## 7. Data Protection\n    85|\n    86|* **Mandatory:** Encrypt in transit and at rest. On every platform in this library this is\n    87|  default behavior — the rule exists so that deviations are deliberate and documented.\n    88|* **Mandatory:** Know where user data physically resides, including data held by third-party\n    89|  processors. This is a §13 question in every blueprint and it must have an answer before\n    90|  first launch, not before first audit.\n    91|* **Mandatory:** Publish a privacy policy before collecting any personal data, including an\n    92|  email address.\n    93|* **Restriction:** Never export production personal data to a personal account, a local\n    94|  machine, or an unmanaged analytics destination.\n    95|\n    96|## 8. Supply Chain\n    97|\n    98|* **Mandatory:** Review dependencies introduced by AI coding assistants before merge. License\n    99|  contamination is silent, and copyleft obligations attach regardless of how the code arrived.\n   100|* **Mandatory:** Establish the project's position on authorship and copyright of\n   101|  AI-generated code before it becomes commercially load-bearing. In some jurisdictions\n   102|  purely machine-generated code attracts no copyright protection, which affects what can be\n   103|  licensed, assigned, or defended. This is a legal determination, not an engineering one —\n   104|  the engineering obligation is to raise it early and record the answer.\n   105|* **Mandatory:** Run dependency vulnerability scanning in CI, and fail the build on\n   106|  known-exploited vulnerabilities.\n   107|* **Recommended:** Pin dependency versions and update deliberately.\n   108|\n   109|## 9. AI and Agent-Specific Controls\n   110|\n   111|Applies to every L3 blueprint and to §7 of every L1/L2 blueprint.\n   112|\n   113|* **Mandatory:** Treat model output as untrusted input. Output that reaches a shell, a query,\n   114|  a filesystem path, or a rendered page passes through the same validation as user input.\n   115|* **Mandatory:** Tools exposed to an agent carry their own authorization. An agent inherits\n   116|  the permissions of its tools, so a broadly-scoped tool grants a broadly-scoped agent.\n   117|* **Mandatory:** Any agent action that is irreversible, outward-facing, or spend-incurring\n   118|  requires either a human approval gate or a hard, tested budget bound.\n   119|* **Mandatory:** Log every tool invocation with its arguments and outcome. An agent without a\n   120|  tool-call audit trail cannot be incident-reviewed.\n   121|* **Restriction:** Never place untrusted retrieved content and privileged instructions in the\n   122|  same trust context without a clear delimiter and an explicit instruction-precedence rule.\n   123|\n   124|## 10. Playbook\n   125|\n   126|1. **Delegate identity on day one.** Retrofitting a managed IdP after launch means migrating\n   127|   live credentials — the most expensive avoidable migration in this library.\n   128|2. **Enforce authorization at the data tier.** Application-tier-only authorization fails open\n   129|   the moment a second consumer of the database appears.\n   130|3. **Assume the client is disclosed.** Design so that full knowledge of the client binary\n   131|   yields no privileged capability.\n   132|4. **Cap spend before shipping the feature.** Rate limits and budget alerts are part of the\n   133|   feature, not follow-up work.\n   134|5. **Wire controls into CI and IaC.** A control enforced by human discipline degrades; a\n   135|   control enforced by a failing build does not.\n   136|\n   137|---\n   138|\n   139|## When This Baseline Is Insufficient\n   140|\n   141|1. Regulated workloads (health, payment, government) — the baseline is a floor, and the\n   142|   applicable regime adds mandatory controls it does not enumerate.\n   143|2. Multi-tenant platforms where tenants are mutually adversarial — tenancy isolation needs\n   144|   dedicated design beyond §12 of a platform blueprint.\n   145|3. Threat models involving insiders or supply-chain compromise of the build system.\n   146|\n   147|---\n   148|\n   149|## 14. Normative Profile\n   150|\n   151|```yaml\n   152|blueprint_id: security-baseline\n   153|blueprint_version: 1.0\n   154|layer: L4\n   155|\n   156|tech_signals:\n   157|  - signal: \"Managed identity provider (platform-specific; see each blueprint §8)\"\n   158|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   159|  - signal: \"Managed secret store (platform-specific; see each blueprint §8)\"\n   160|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   161|  - signal: \"WAF with managed rule sets at every public entry point\"\n   162|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   163|\n   164|standards:\n   165|  - area: security\n   166|    rule: \"Delegate authentication to a managed identity provider; never implement password storage, reset, or session issuance in application code.\"\n   167|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   168|  - area: security\n   169|    rule: \"Enforce authorization server-side or in the data tier; client-side role checks carry no security weight.\"\n   170|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   171|  - area: security\n   172|    rule: \"Enable row-level authorization on every table holding user data, not only tables currently queried from the client.\"\n   173|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   174|  - area: security\n   175|    rule: \"Validate and sanitize every input server-side for type, length, range, and format.\"\n   176|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   177|  - area: security\n   178|    rule: \"Return generic error messages with a correlation id; never expose stack traces, table names, column names, or query fragments to a client.\"\n   179|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   180|  - area: security\n   181|    rule: \"Keep secret keys server-side, injected from a managed secret store; only publishable keys may ship to clients.\"\n   182|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   183|  - area: security\n   184|    rule: \"Rotate any key suspected of exposure immediately, before assessing impact.\"\n   185|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   186|  - area: security\n   187|    rule: \"Route all client AI calls through a server-side proxy holding the model credential; model keys never reach a mobile binary or web bundle.\"\n   188|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   189|  - area: security\n   190|    rule: \"Rate-limit every endpoint reaching a metered third-party API, and set hard spend caps with alerts below the cap.\"\n   191|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   192|  - area: security\n   193|    rule: \"Restrict CORS to known origins in production.\"\n   194|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   195|  - area: security\n   196|    rule: \"Treat model output as untrusted input; validate before it reaches a shell, query, filesystem path, or rendered page.\"\n   197|    evidence: \"§9\"\n   198|  - area: security\n   199|    rule: \"Require a human approval gate or a tested budget bound for any agent action that is irreversible, outward-facing, or spend-incurring.\"\n   200|    evidence: \"§9\"\n   201|  - area: security\n   202|    rule: \"Log every agent tool invocation with arguments and outcome.\"\n   203|    evidence: \"§9\"\n   204|  - area: test\n   205|    rule: \"Test authorization policies by attempting access as a different user and as an unauthenticated caller.\"\n   206|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   207|  - area: automation\n   208|    rule: \"Run dependency vulnerability scanning in CI and fail the build on known-exploited vulnerabilities.\"\n   209|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   210|  - area: automation\n   211|    rule: \"Review dependencies introduced by AI coding assistants before merge, for license contamination.\"\n   212|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   213|  - area: automation\n   214|    rule: \"Pin dependency versions and update deliberately.\"\n   215|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   216|\n   217|constraints:\n   218|  - constraint: \"The project's position on authorship and copyright of AI-generated code must be determined and recorded before that code becomes commercially load-bearing; in some jurisdictions purely machine-generated code attracts no copyright protection.\"\n   219|    source: \"§8\"\n   220|  - constraint: \"A privacy policy must exist before any personal data — including an email address — is collected.\"\n   221|    source: \"§7\"\n   222|  - constraint: \"Data residency for user data, including third-party processors, must be documented before first launch.\"\n   223|    source: \"§7\"\n   224|  - constraint: \"No secret may exist anywhere in version-control history.\"\n   225|    source: \"§5\"\n   226|\n   227|infra:\n   228|  - item: \"Secret store\"\n   229|    detail: \"Managed; secrets injected at runtime, never committed. Platform-specific implementation in each blueprint §8.\"\n   230|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   231|  - item: \"WAF / edge\"\n   232|    detail: \"Managed rule sets in front of every public entry point.\"\n   233|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   234|  - item: \"Spend controls\"\n   235|    detail: \"Hard cap plus alert threshold on every metered provider.\"\n   236|    evidence: \"§6\"\n   237|\n   238|commands:\n   239|  - purpose: security\n   240|    command: \"<project dependency-audit command> — wired into CI, non-zero exit fails the build\"\n   241|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   242|\n   243|open_questions:\n   244|  - question: \"Which regulatory regime applies, and what does it add beyond this baseline?\"\n   245|    why: \"Determined by customer jurisdiction and sector, not by platform choice.\"\n   246|  - question: \"Are tenants mutually adversarial?\"\n   247|    why: \"Changes the tenancy isolation requirement in §12 of the selected platform blueprint.\"\n   248|  - question: \"What are the actual spend caps per metered provider?\"\n   249|    why: \"Derived from project budget, not from platform capability.\"\n   250|```\n</file>","isError":false}}
