{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01LukD6RNhhcWaJDWWwtC6uC","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.ai_pods_stepwise/registry/skills/tech-blueprints/blueprints/l4-concerns/pre-ship-checklist.md\" lines=\"204\" start=\"1\" end=\"203\" returned=\"203\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Cross-Cutting Concern: Pre-Ship Checklist\n     2|\n     3|> **Thesis** — Verification gates, not architecture. What must be *checked* before a release,\n     4|> as distinct from what must be *built*, which is the job of the blueprints.\n     5|> **Applies to** — every project, regardless of platform.\n     6|> **Artifact type** — checklist. Deliberately not merged into any blueprint; blueprints answer\n     7|> *what to build*, this answers *what to verify*.\n     8|\n     9|---\n    10|\n    11|## 1. Executive Summary\n    12|\n    13|Every item here is a gate with a pass/fail outcome, ordered so that cheap checks run before\n    14|expensive ones and automated checks run before human ones. The three layers — manual probes,\n    15|assisted review, and automated scanning — are complementary. None is sufficient alone:\n    16|scanners miss business-logic and authorization-state bugs; humans miss cross-cutting\n    17|configuration drift; assisted review finds a wide but shallow band between them.\n    18|\n    19|This document feeds `test-standards.md` (quality gates) and `validation-tools.md` (command\n    20|registry) in the generated context pack.\n    21|\n    22|## 2. Gate 0 — Automated, Blocking\n    23|\n    24|Runs in CI on every pull request. A failure blocks merge.\n    25|\n    26|| Check | Pass condition |\n    27||---|---|\n    28|| Build | Clean build for every target in the workspace |\n    29|| Typecheck | Zero errors; no suppression added in the diff without justification |\n    30|| Lint and format | Zero errors; formatting is machine-enforced, never reviewed by humans |\n    31|| Unit tests | Pass; coverage does not decrease against the base branch |\n    32|| Integration tests | Pass against ephemeral infrastructure, not a shared environment |\n    33|| Dependency audit | No known-exploited vulnerabilities; no new copyleft license without approval |\n    34|| Secret scan | No credential pattern anywhere in the diff or in history |\n    35|| IaC plan | Plan is clean and reviewed; no out-of-band resource drift |\n    36|\n    37|## 3. Gate 1 — Auth Failure Paths (Manual, Per Release)\n    38|\n    39|Happy-path authentication is covered by integration tests. These are the paths that are\n    40|routinely missed, each of which is an information-disclosure or availability defect.\n    41|\n    42|- [ ] Repeated failed logins rate-limit or lock, and the response does **not** reveal whether\n    43|      the account exists.\n    44|- [ ] Password reset for a non-existent address returns the same response as for an existing\n    45|      one, with the same timing characteristics.\n    46|- [ ] Signing up with an already-registered address does not disclose that the address is\n    47|      registered.\n    48|- [ ] Double-clicking an email verification link degrades gracefully; the second use does not\n    49|      error visibly or invalidate the session.\n    50|- [ ] An expired or already-consumed reset token is rejected with a generic message.\n    51|- [ ] Session revocation takes effect immediately on the server, not merely in the client.\n    52|- [ ] Access as a *different authenticated user* is denied at the data tier, verified by\n    53|      direct query — not only through the application.\n    54|- [ ] Access as an *unauthenticated* caller to every endpoint that should require auth is\n    55|      denied.\n    56|\n    57|## 4. Gate 2 — Assisted Review (Per Release)\n    58|\n    59|Run against the codebase with a coding assistant. Each is a distinct pass; combining them\n    60|into one prompt reliably degrades recall.\n    61|\n    62|1. Review security headers and baseline HTTP posture.\n    63|2. Review against OWASP Top 10, naming the specific file and line for each finding.\n    64|3. Search for credential or personal-data leakage in frontend code, API responses, and log\n    65|   output.\n    66|4. Confirm no API key, token, or connection string is reachable from client code or visible\n    67|   in a network call.\n    68|5. Review authorization: enumerate every data-tier policy and identify tables with none.\n    69|6. For AI features: review prompt-construction sites for untrusted-input interpolation, and\n    70|   tool definitions for over-broad scope.\n    71|\n    72|Findings are triaged, not auto-applied. Assisted review produces false positives at a rate\n    73|that makes blind application harmful.\n    74|\n    75|## 5. Gate 3 — Operational Readiness\n    76|\n    77|- [ ] Rate limits are configured on every endpoint reaching a metered third-party API.\n    78|- [ ] Hard spend caps and alert thresholds are set on every metered provider, with the alert\n    79|      meaningfully below the cap.\n    80|- [ ] CORS is restricted to production origins.\n    81|- [ ] WAF is enabled with managed rules on every public entry point.\n    82|- [ ] Error responses in production are sanitized; verified by triggering a real failure.\n    83|- [ ] Logs contain no personal data or credentials; verified by sampling.\n    84|- [ ] Rollback path is tested, not merely documented.\n    85|- [ ] Alerting reaches a human who is on duty; verified by firing a test alert.\n    86|\n    87|## 6. Gate 4 — Legal and Data\n    88|\n    89|- [ ] Privacy policy is published and accurate about what is collected and where it resides.\n    90|- [ ] Data residency is documented for the primary datastore and every third-party processor.\n    91|- [ ] Dependency licenses are reviewed, including transitive dependencies added by AI\n    92|      assistants during the release window.\n    93|- [ ] Data deletion and export paths exist if the applicable regime requires them.\n    94|\n    95|## 7. Gate 5 — Final Automated Scan\n    96|\n    97|A dedicated security scanner as the last step before deploy. This catches cross-cutting\n    98|issues that per-file review misses. It does **not** replace Gates 1–4: a clean scan alongside\n    99|an untested authorization policy is a false-confidence result, which is worse than no scan.\n   100|\n   101|## 8. Playbook\n   102|\n   103|1. **Automate every gate that can be automated.** A checklist item enforced by human\n   104|   discipline degrades within three releases.\n   105|2. **Run failure paths, not just happy paths.** Every item in Gate 1 exists because the happy\n   106|   path passed while the failure path disclosed information.\n   107|3. **Triage assisted-review findings; never auto-apply.** False-positive rates make blind\n   108|   application a net negative.\n   109|4. **Treat a clean scan as necessary, not sufficient.** Business-logic and authorization-state\n   110|   defects are invisible to scanners by construction.\n   111|5. **Gate the release, not the developer.** Gates belong in CI and in the release process,\n   112|   where they are uniform, not in individual review habits.\n   113|\n   114|---\n   115|\n   116|## When This Checklist Is Insufficient\n   117|\n   118|1. Regulated releases requiring formal evidence, sign-off, and retention.\n   119|2. Systems where an outage is a safety event rather than an availability event.\n   120|3. First release of a multi-tenant platform — tenancy isolation needs dedicated adversarial\n   121|   testing beyond Gate 1.\n   122|\n   123|---\n   124|\n   125|## 14. Normative Profile\n   126|\n   127|```yaml\n   128|blueprint_id: pre-ship-checklist\n   129|blueprint_version: 1.0\n   130|layer: L4\n   131|\n   132|standards:\n   133|  - area: test\n   134|    rule: \"Every pull request must pass build, typecheck, lint, unit tests, integration tests, dependency audit, secret scan, and IaC plan review before merge.\"\n   135|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   136|  - area: test\n   137|    rule: \"Coverage must not decrease relative to the base branch.\"\n   138|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   139|  - area: test\n   140|    rule: \"Integration tests run against ephemeral infrastructure, never a shared environment.\"\n   141|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   142|  - area: test\n   143|    rule: \"Every release must verify the eight auth failure paths in Gate 1, including data-tier access as a different authenticated user.\"\n   144|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   145|  - area: test\n   146|    rule: \"Assisted-review findings are triaged by a human and never auto-applied.\"\n   147|    evidence: \"§4\"\n   148|  - area: test\n   149|    rule: \"Rollback path must be tested before release, not only documented.\"\n   150|    evidence: \"§5\"\n   151|  - area: test\n   152|    rule: \"A final automated security scan runs as the last step before deploy and does not substitute for Gates 1-4.\"\n   153|    evidence: \"§7\"\n   154|  - area: automation\n   155|    rule: \"Formatting is machine-enforced and never subject to human review.\"\n   156|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   157|  - area: automation\n   158|    rule: \"All release gates execute in CI, not as individual reviewer habits.\"\n   159|    evidence: \"§8\"\n   160|  - area: security\n   161|    rule: \"Authentication failure responses must not disclose account existence, including via response timing.\"\n   162|    evidence: \"§3\"\n   163|\n   164|constraints:\n   165|  - constraint: \"A failing Gate 0 check blocks merge; gates are not advisory.\"\n   166|    source: \"§2\"\n   167|  - constraint: \"Privacy policy, data residency documentation, and license review must complete before release.\"\n   168|    source: \"§6\"\n   169|\n   170|commands:\n   171|  - purpose: build\n   172|    command: \"<project build command>\"\n   173|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   174|  - purpose: typecheck\n   175|    command: \"<project typecheck command>\"\n   176|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   177|  - purpose: lint\n   178|    command: \"<project lint command>\"\n   179|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   180|  - purpose: test\n   181|    command: \"<project unit test command>\"\n   182|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   183|  - purpose: test\n   184|    command: \"<project integration test command, targeting ephemeral infrastructure>\"\n   185|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   186|  - purpose: security\n   187|    command: \"<project dependency audit command>\"\n   188|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   189|  - purpose: security\n   190|    command: \"<project secret scan command>\"\n   191|    evidence: \"§2\"\n   192|  - purpose: security\n   193|    command: \"<final pre-deploy security scan>\"\n   194|    evidence: \"§7\"\n   195|\n   196|open_questions:\n   197|  - question: \"What are the concrete commands for build, test, lint, typecheck, and security in this project?\"\n   198|    why: \"Stack- and repository-specific; resolved from the selected L2 blueprint and the project's manifests.\"\n   199|  - question: \"What coverage threshold applies?\"\n   200|    why: \"Set per project; this document mandates only that coverage must not regress.\"\n   201|  - question: \"Does the applicable regime require formal release evidence and retention?\"\n   202|    why: \"Determined by customer sector and jurisdiction.\"\n   203|```\n</file>","isError":false}}
